{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf","id":1521708,"title":"Thrassus/Exhibit #338 as an enemy faction in Werewolf","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-08-30T18:02:20-07:00","id":1521708,"title":"Thrassus/Exhibit #338 as an enemy faction in Werewolf","content":"Time to move on from vampires.<br /> <br /> I like creating distinct \u201cfactions\u201d of the Wyrm because it introduces much needed variety in terms of antagonists. The way banes and fomori are usually presented, it is easy to think of them as generic and therefore lacking in interesting details. Building a faction around a great tentpole and building on that tentpole\u2019s theme is important to how I run chronicles. Exhibit # 338 the Anti-Christ Serum is a Weaver fetish described in the Book of the Weaver. It is a fetish prison that might be holding one of the Talons of the Wyrm, Thrassus the Thrice Damned. I also want to point out that while we, the readers, are told of the (possible) association of Exhibit <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/node/338\" >#</a> 338 and Thrassus, this does not seem to be known to <i>anyone</i> IC.<br /> <br /> The Talons of the Wyrm were described in apocalyptic imagery when they were mentioned, yet somehow never became a major focus of the game. These should have been the basis of major Wyrm factions, not trivia or afterthoughts. So we have to do better. This thread is about using Exhibit # 338 to develop a full blown Wyrm faction STs can use in their chronicles.<br /> <br /> I think the Talons are a vastly underutilized enemy in Werewolf. While their names get mentioned (especially in early edition), the Talons don\u2019t seem to ever drive anything itself. Instead, they are just plot coupons for some NPCs to look for, these NPCs don\u2019t have strong connections to the Talon, and the forces of the Talon are just an individual NPC. You could easily switch out the Talon for some other plot coupon of the ST\u2019s choice. To me, if you have a concept like the Talons of the Wyrm, there should be recognizable factions around them. Rage Across Russia gave the most details of any Talon, but there Koschei was clearly subservient to Baba Yaga.<br /> <br /> I think a key element is that any Wyrm faction needs to be somehow relevant in multiple eras and locations. Pentex is fine, but is limited by it only able to exist in the 20<sup>th</sup> century. Something like the Talons of the Wyrm should be found throughout historical eras and multiple human cultures. So we should keep this in mind when developing our ideas.\u200b<br /> <br /> Exhibit # 338 is clearly based on John Carpenter\u2019s 1987 film Prince of Darkness. It even gets referenced in-character as \u201cThe Anti-Christ Serum\u201d is presumably the title of a similar movie by \u201cWes Carpenter\u201d (a portmanteau of Wes Craven and John Carpenter). So we can start there as inspiration.<br /> <br /> And I mean just inspiration. We need to start somewhere, but we should not be limited to what is just in Prince of Darkness. The needs of an RPG is different from any movie, and adapting something to a new setting requires more. But I think it is a good beginning so let\u2019s move on. Also, if you have ideas on a different direction be sure to post too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-30T18:06:55-07:00","id":1521709,"title":"","content":"I do not find Prince of Darkness to be a very good movie, but it does have a cult following. Like most John Carpenter movies, it has a lot of evocative, creative, and intriguing ideas in it. For those that do not know, despite some religious trappings in the movie, this is not religious horror. It is cosmic horror based very much on the fringes of science and the unknown of a materialist, reductionist universe. But if you have not seen the movie, there will be spoilers, so decide whether you want to continue.<br /> <br /> These videos provide some important information for those who have not seen it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XDUoNHSceAM\" data-code=\"XDUoNHSceAM\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/XDUoNHSceAM/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HYZIU1uIS2w\" data-code=\"HYZIU1uIS2w\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/HYZIU1uIS2w/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> What are the elements of Carpenter\u2019s Prince of Darkness that are most relevant? I would pick the following:<ul><li>a terrifying and powerful entity is trapped within a device</li> <li>the device, the liquid inside it, and its effects on the outside world are in defiance of physics</li> <li>the entity is the essence of ultimate evil</li> <li>dreams an important medium to communicate to characters about the entity (albeit these dreams are not from the entity, but other people in the future attempting to warn others)</li> <li>the device appears to be not so much a prison as a timebomb that will go off based on certain celestial events</li> <li>once the celestial event happens, there is a noticeable, but undescribable changes in earth and sky</li> <li>mirrors can be used as a gateway to another dimension where even more dangerous entity is imprisoned</li> <li>the entity, once it is partially released, is able to possess or take over the mind of people with weak willpower (in the movie this was nearby homeless people)</li> <li>the entity seems to be able to control the natural forces of decay and degeneration in this world such as insects</li> <li>the entity is able to seize outright control of people that become in physical contact with the tainted fluid that leaks out from the device</li> <li>the entity inside the device (\u201cthe anti-christ\u201d) will free another, more powerful entity trapped in the other dimension (\u201csatan\u201d)</li> <li>this other entity has destroyed other worlds before arriving on this one</li> <li>the other entity\u2019s arrival on Earth will herald the end of humanity</li> </ul>You might come up with a different list than me, and that's fine. But this is what I'll go off of.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-30T18:12:05-07:00","id":1521710,"title":"","content":"There\u2019s obviously a lot relevant to Werewolf in general and the Talons of the Wyrm in specific. And while we do not need to directly translate these elements into Werewolf, there is a lot of inspiration to steal.<br /> <br /> The Weaver device/fetish is obviously a prison. Similar to how the meteorite in Rite of Passage is a prison for Narlthus (and all the Talons are somehow imprisoned). So instead of two entities, there\u2019s just one - Thrassus.<br /> <br /> While Prince of Darkness gives it own explanation for the obvious technological prison (it was built by space aliens), that is not really an option for Exhibit # 338. So the Weaver device is much more anomalous. (Though to be honest despite calling it Weaver technology, it just appears to be a large glass container whose glass appears indestructible and does not react to the outside environment. That\u2019s not what I normally think of when something is called a Weaver device. It might simply be considered a \u201cWeaver device\u201d because it seems to be in the hands of some organization which is either science based or possibly the actual Technocracy). An ST can either decide to keep that a mystery (which can be effective to players, though I personally dislike mysteries the ST herself doesn\u2019t even know) or come up with some explanation that somehow makes sense to them (regardless of whether the PCs ever discover that).<br /> <br /> The dreams of Thrassus contained inside the prison shoudl obviously communicate its desires to the world around it. Narlthus\u2019 prison was pretty much the same way in corrupting spirits, animals, and people around it in order to free it, and this included the Black Spiral Dancer Azarea in Rite of Passage who had ongoing dreams that propelled her to find Narlthus. So that matches well.<br /> <br /> However, instead of it being a time bomb meant to go off at a certain time, perhaps there are certain celestial events (that will happen at the time of the Apocalypse - something similar to the Red Star) which will allow Thrassus to be free. Unlike Narlthus\u2019s prison, it is not possible to free the Talon earlier. Unfortunately, that also means it is inevitable that Thrassus will be released (at the worst time possible) and the Garou cannot prevent it.<br /> <br /> It is foretold that Thrassus will herald the end of humanity. But it isn\u2019t quite clear when this happen or whether that can be prevented. It might just be an event that will only happen in the late stages of the Apocalypse after lots of other things have gone wrong. Or that the Garou (or others) might still be able to prevent this should they win the final battle. Or that this is just somnething that will happen on its own in a long distant future of geological time. Maybe humanity will survive, but in a different form; or that it won\u2019t be limited to humanity? This shouldn\u2019t be something relevant to most chronicles, just a terrifying flavor to the faction. In various Lovecraft stories, there are sometimes mentions that the earth is to be cleared off or similar. I like the idea that one of Thrassus\u2019 (presumed) roles will be to be clear humanity from Gaia, perhaps in preparation of some event or arrival.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dunwich Horror</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">They from the air told me at Sabbat that it will be years<b> before I can clear off the earth</b>, and I guess grandfather will be dead then, so I shall have to learn all the angles of the planes and all the formulas between the Yr and the Nhhngr. They from outside will help, but they cannot take body without human blood. That upstairs looks it will have the right cast. I can see it a little when I make the Voorish sign or blow the powder of Ibn Ghazi at it, and it is near like them at May-Eve on the Hill. The other face may wear off some. I wonder how I shall look <b>when the earth is cleared and there are no earth beings on it.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>\u200b<br /> <br /> We have some direct correspondences to concepts like fomori created by Thrassus. So that is an easy and obvious match. We\u2019ll talk more about this in a later post.<br /> <br /> I will use the above as the basis for Thrassus and its fetish prison. So how do we go about building an actual faction around this, since the Talon itself (as a character) will likely never appear in game?<br /> <br /> The one thing I know is that I want any cult of Thrassus to be a terrifying opponent. I don\u2019t mean from a combat perspective necessarily. I mean from a lore perspective and how Garou should perceive the threat of Thrassus and its thralls. As a Talon of the Wyrm, it should be seen with a healthy bit of trepidation, concern, and alarm. It should have some perceived role in the Apocalypse and an accompanying dread as a result. Its minions may not necessarily be any more difficult to fight or disgusting to witness as other Wyrm minions, but they should be distinguishable from other factions and have an identifable theme. While this faction in the pre-apocalypse may not be too terrible, there should be a sense that it will become very powerful once Thrassus escapes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-30T18:13:13-07:00","id":1521711,"title":"","content":"I\u2019ll posting more soon, but don\u2019t want to overwhelm the beginning of a thread with so many posts from one person. So I'll leave it open to comments for now.<br /> <br /> = = = = =<br /> I won\u2019t be describing a full blown faction here. I\u2019ll just be posting some ideas, and hope others will post their ideas as well. And then we\u2019ll try to cobble things together. When we talked about Odin and Set, both already had a lot of established lore in the game as well as mythology in general. That gave us a solid foundation upon which to build. But with Thrassus, we are much more bare bones so there will be a lot of brainstorming at first.<br /> <br /> First, I think there are some obvious fomori and banes that we can connect to Thrassus inspired from the above. This is just to get us started on what the factiion may be like. Right now this is just brainstorming, so other ideas are welcome.<br /> <br /> There are some ties to Scavenger Pack Banes and the Hollow Men fomori. Vermin infested fomori wearing a human skin suit is a very visceral image. It is a horrible image of entropy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gc2ohLzoaOo\" data-code=\"gc2ohLzoaOo\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/gc2ohLzoaOo/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iY-QBvy3lR8\" data-code=\"iY-QBvy3lR8\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/iY-QBvy3lR8/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> There are some specifics about Hollow Men that don\u2019t fit well with our theme, but we can make the necessary changes for this variant. We can say that the presence of Thrassus corrupted/changed the nature of those Scavenger Pack banes drawn to him. Maybe restrict them to just insect types and only use the Poison Spider colony version. Replace the Prolonged Life and Scent of the Wyld powers with more suitable powers, possibly Animal Control (limited to insects) and Roar of the Wyrm.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 09-02-2023, 02:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-01T23:12:28-07:00","id":1521856,"title":"","content":"I also like the Puppeteer Banes mentioned in Valkenburg Foundation. For some reason, these banes were never mentioned again in future sourcebooks like Book of the Wyrm so they have been forgotten. These banes can be a great tool for the ST, so it\u2019s bad so few know about them because of the relative obscurity of that early sourcebook.<br /> <br /> Puppeteer Banes basically take control of people while they sleep and do various things to further the cause of the Wyrm. But they do not normally turn people into fomori. Instead, they just use people as unknowing puppets like they are in a trance. This fits very well with what we see in Prince of Darkness.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CdFL2wBVKG0\" data-code=\"CdFL2wBVKG0\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/CdFL2wBVKG0/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=beP_PTjeqkw\" data-code=\"beP_PTjeqkw\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/beP_PTjeqkw/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> \u200b<br /> I also think given the idea that the laws of physics are being defied that the Warp Reality charm should be involved. I think Nexus Crawlers are the only banes shown to have it. But Nexus Crawlers are very powerful on their own, and are already canonically associated with G\u2019lough. So I think we\u2019d need to create a new bane with the charm that is much less powerful and specifically tied to Thrassus. I want to see noticeable entropic effects that are impossible but not as powerful as what the Nexus Crawlers can do. Maybe limit these spirits to only 3 Willpower to limit its possible effects to the environment, and not actual characters themselves. I think that fits very well with the idea that there are \u201cchanges in the earth and sky\u201d. Maybe subtle things that aren\u2019t recognized at first, but once they are become disturbing. Not just for the changes themselves, but the realization by Garou that it means the powers of Thrassus is active among them. But we will need to come up with some imagery and flavor text to create this new bane.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-01T23:16:01-07:00","id":1521857,"title":"","content":"I also think some Nihilarchs are appropriate. They are found in the W20 Book of the Wyrm and are said to appear at portentous moments in the war against Gaia, usually moments when a significant piece of the Mother is about to die. That sounds appropriate to the nihilistic horror of Thrassus and hints at its possible role in the setting.<br /> <br /> I am also thinking if we want to keep some kind of Anti-Christ theme, that having some Breeder Banes and Ferectoi fomori associated to Thrassus (though these banes/fomori are not unique to him) would be good. There is nothing in Prince of Darkness like this, but there is an Anti-Christ theme to that in both the movie and the name of the Weaver device. We could call them something like the Seed of Thrassus. These Ferectoi could be the leaders of the cult actively striving to fulfill the goals of Thrassus whereas the other spirits and fomori seem to be more like pawns. This gives us cult leaders and groups that actual packs of Garou PCs can fight.<br /> <br /> This is what immediately comes to mind to me, but we\u2019re not limited to that. We will add more to this later.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-03T22:42:17-07:00","id":1522034,"title":"","content":"I want to describe now the Ferectoi that could be associated with this. Ferectoi are more like a concept than a fully described fomori. As written, Ferectoi are mentioned to be very individual with unique powers given to each one. They are not one size fits all, but supposed to be very customizable.<br /> <br /> So the Ferectoi of Thrassus won\u2019t be like Damien from the Omen movies. I think they should be more like the Spawn of Nyogtha in the Call of Cthulhu RPG. Those are offspring between humans and the Great Old One Nyogtha. They are born looking human, but sometime between their thirties and sixties they degenerate into shapeless abominations much like Nyogtha itself. While initially just like human children (except that they have viscuous black ichor instead of blood), around puberty Nyogtha fills their dreams with his thoughts and visions, and these hybrids eventually succumb to Nyogtha\u2019s will. They then seduce others into their dark god\u2019s cult.<br /> <br /> They\u2019ve only appeared in a few scenarios, but are mentioned in any of the big monster compilations for CoC. There is a good summary of everything Nyogtha in the CoC RPG on this blog post.<br /> <br /> <u><a href=\"https://mdcls.blogspot.com/2015/07/things-that-should-not-be.html\" target=\"_blank\">https://mdcls.blogspot.com/2015/07/t...ld-not-be.html</a></u><br /> <br /> I think that fits perfectly for these Ferectoi. They\u2019ll have several decades of appearing human after they fall to Thrassus\u2019s will, maybe some years afterwards where they degenerate but an still disguise themself, and then afterwards they are just horrid, inhuman looking monsters that can hide under the earth, waiting for the Apocalypse and perhaps ruling some of the Thrassus cults behind the scenes.<br /> <br /> This does not mean all Thrassus Ferectoi have the exact same powers. But there should be a variety of powers that overlap between them (providing a common theme), and then some level of specific customization.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-07T20:24:36-07:00","id":1522253,"title":"","content":"Now that we have some idea of who or what makes up the faction, we can figure out what that faction may be like. The movie doesn\u2019t show any long standing cult. But the idea of an ancient evil, slumbering until certain celestial events happen, and its cult being drawn to it by dreams have some obvious antecedents. The most prominent in my mind is the cult of Cthulhu as described in HP Lovecraft\u2019s story The Call of Cthulhu, but it's an old trope by now and plenty of other examples.<br /> <br /> This youtube video does a good quick overview of the main points. But like with the movie of Prince of Darkness, we shouldn't be looking to duplicate things exactly, just draw from it for inspiration. We should always try to put our own spin on things.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fJFhAQMFpiU\" data-code=\"fJFhAQMFpiU\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/fJFhAQMFpiU/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> The cult is formed mostly by those minds weak and sensitive enough to receive the dreams of Thrassus and be influenced by them. The cult, therefore, is probably mostly made up of dregs. That\u2019s fine because Ferectoi can provide any leadership needed for the cult. This doesn\u2019t mean ordinary cultists are imbeciles or too insane to do anything, but they probably aren\u2019t the kind of people others are going to choose to follow. They are not on top of the social hierarchy. This does not mean that only dregs receive such dreams, just that those of stronger will, character, or intent won\u2019t be influenced by them. Individual reactions to these dream sendings are varied. Neither does it mean that anyone actually understands these dream sendings or what Thrassus may actually want. They just experience them and come to their own conclusions.<br /> <br /> The cult is ancient, going all the way back shortly after the Talons were defeated and imprisoned. These cultists have existed since the Stone Age before the Impergium was ever instituted. So the Garou have had long experience fighting them, just as they have experience fighting the Garou. Even if human cultists by themselves cannot pass on such knowledge to the next generation of the cult, the banes associated with the cult will remember.<br /> <br /> The cult is worldwide because the dreams of Thrassus can reach around the world. Thus despite the cult appearing in a diverse variety of human cultures, there is remarkable uniformity to the cult in its practices, imagery, and rites because they all get it first hand from Thrassus. So the outer appearance of the cult might be different in China versus Europe versus Peru versus Arabia, but pierce the surface and the signs are there.<br /> <br /> Although the cult was contacted by Thrassus through dreams, he isn\u2019t actually commanding them to do anything. He\u2019s in slumber within the fetish prison. Cultists are influenced by Thrassus, but he is not actively instructing them. So while they attempt to please him, it isn\u2019t clear what does please Thrassus although after some twelve thousand years or more, individual cult groups have their own ideas. It is actually the Ferectoi that provide any kind of instructions (although this too are probably just guesses than true knowledge).<br /> <br /> The cult\u2019s practices are loathsome and aberrant, and thus relegated to the fringes of society where it is easier for cultists to hide their crimes and not attract attention. Between PENTEX, vampires, Seventh Generation, and many other groups that operate in the socio-economic heights, I think it\u2019d be interesting to approach things from a different angle. The threat of Thrassus comes from places where you expect powerlessness.<br /> <br /> The cult looks forward to the day when Thrassus awakens once again because he can then formally instruct his disciples in all manner of things so mere men could become like Thrassus. Then they\u2019ll learn secret knowledge, arcane power, new ways to shout, kill, rape, and pillage - to be beyond good and evil like Thrassus itself. Then Thrassus would rule the earth, or whatever is left of it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-09T23:01:00-07:00","id":1522330,"title":"","content":"We need something more. This is Werewolf after all, and we need some kind of physical threat to the Garou. So this is an area where we have to look beyond Prince of Darkness while hoping what we\u2019ve gleaned can point to a productive direction.<br /> <br /> One area where I want to look are the works of William Hope Hodgson. His works display the kind of hostile universe that Thrassus implies. It also has a setting where the normal laws of physics seem to degrade and breakdown in the presence of those hostile forces in works like The Nightland, The House on the Borderland, and the cases of Carnacki: Ghost Finder. That kind of entropy breaking down physical, spiritual, and human laws fits the theme of Thrassus.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e9UHSUhkLiM\" data-code=\"e9UHSUhkLiM\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/e9UHSUhkLiM/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> A common element in Carnack\u2019s horror stories are terrifying pig-like creatures. They show up in both House on the Borderland and the Carnack story The Hog. I mention it because it also shows up here, and I don\u2019t think there was no inspiration of one on the other.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tnU7zf9YVoA\" data-code=\"tnU7zf9YVoA\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/tnU7zf9YVoA/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> This is something I think resonates with the savage horror of Werewolf: the Apocalypse. But they can\u2019t be like orcs in a fantasy world. The World of Darkness still needs to be similar in appearance to our own. Pig monsters can\u2019t be showing up in downtown Chicago or besieging a small town. So I would say the Swine Things are not inhabitants of our world. But extra-dimensional alien invaders from outside reality are good. They are attracted to the \u201cpsychic energy\u201d of humanity. They feed on that, not material flesh and blood.<br /> <br /> So if they are not from our world, where do the Garou encounter them? I think Chimares are a good place. In the Hodgson story the Hog, it was in a victim\u2019s dreams where he was being psychically devoured. Perhaps we extend this to them devouring other forms of psychic energy like Gnosis as well so they are a threat to other spirits. But they have a particular taste for the human psyche. While we may want to keep them reminiscent of pigs, they are obviously not pigs or pig spirits. Just piglike. So when naming them, we my want to avoid including anything like pig or swine, just to make clear to the players that this is something unnatural and not native to earth. These are not corrupted Gaian spirits. Of course, if you don\u2019t like swine imagery yourself, you can change their appearance to something else. While I like that imagery myself, I would not want that to be the identifiable thing about them, so I\u2019ll use a more netural name for them like Gnostic Marauders.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-09T23:05:08-07:00","id":1522331,"title":"","content":"Going with the idea that Thrassus is such an abomination its presence can distort laws of physics and the laws of Gaia, maybe there are parts of the earth that are so impacted. And that these areas can produce aberrations that should not exist - thus forms certain kinds of penumbral Wyrm domains, although I don\u2019t think they should be wandering far from their domains in the Penumbra itself. Just at special extra-dimensional fault lines that lead to cracks in reality, perhaps partially dependent on certain celestial events so the threat comes and goes. Garou just can\u2019t dogpile on known Thrassus domains because these domains never stay long. They arrive, cause havoc, and then suddenly disappear. I think that goes a long way at creating the sense the Garou are not in control ofthe situation. Yeah, they can congratulate themselves that the local threat is \u201cgone\u201d, everyone knows the Garou had nothing to do with it leaving.<br /> <br /> Somewhere out there in the Dream Zone is the so-called Dream House of Thrassus. That is not where Thrassus is from, but it\u2019s the terrifying dreamscape that embodies the collective madness of Thrassus\u2019s outreach to the sleeping minds of its earthly minions and victims. This is where the Chimares and Domains connect to. Tribal legends of the Stargazers and Uktena have the names of brave Garou whose packs voyaged to the Dream House of Thrassus in the hopes of learning information on their foe. None have come back. Because such Dream Realms can border and intersect the Far Dreaming of Changeling, on rare occasions the Gnostic Marauders manage to invade the Dreaming itself. When this happens, the Fae always call packs of Stag to help.<br /> <br /> As for the Penumbral Domains themselves, some kind of ruins or underground tunnel imagery works well as that calls back to the Hodgson stories and Darkest Dungeon. But again, you may want something more imaginative.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-24T16:48:42-07:00","id":1523103,"title":"","content":"We also need some more opponents in the physical world, especially some thing that can provide some kind of physical challenge to the Garou. They don\u2019t need to be as physically tough as Garou, just enough that players can enjoy the fight. We do have the Hollow Men fomori already, but I think we need something else as well as those fomori are not noteworthy fighters.<br /> <br /> My inspirations here go back to the viscuous green liquid contained in the device in Prince of Darkness. One aspect of Prince of Darkness is the green liquid inside leaving its prison and infecting others. It\u2019s a strong image, and I\u2019d like to use it. That first brings to mind the fomori power Viscuous Form, which is a power I\u2019ve never used before in game. Viscuous Form then brings to mind gelatinous horror monsters found in Call of Cthulhu like the Formless Spawn of Tsathoggua. And mention of Tsathoggua reminds me of a story Clark Ashton Smith wrote that included the figure Knygathin Zhaum. He was a human looking monster which would come back from the dead as strange liquid pools from his body would reform into more monstrous versions that look less and less human. That\u2019s a good horror image and would be a good basis for a fomori. That story can be found here:<br /> <br /> <u><a href=\"http://www.eldritchdark.com/writings/short-stories/210/the-testament-of-athammaus\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.eldritchdark.com/writings...t-of-athammaus</a></u><br /> <br /> These would be very similar to the Ferectoi based on the Spawn of Nyotha, but perhaps a bit more physically powerful, less intelligent, and not as long lived. Good disposable henchmen types for the Ferectoi. So what kind of powers would be good to give this kind of fomori? Going with the disgusting liquid theme, here are the fomori powers that seem most evocative to me. We may want to only use some of them and not all of them.<ul><li>Viscuous Form</li> <li>Noxious Miasma</li> <li>Slobbersnot</li> <li>Tar Skin</li> <li>Toxic Secretions</li> </ul>I also like the idea that perhaps the fomori degenerates over time into a more liquid, plastic form as it takes damage or perhaps is \u201ckilled\u201d. Maybe that its own unique power or some variant of the Regeneration power. These are not very offensive minded powers with the possible exception of Toxic Secretions (but perhaps combined with Tar Skin, it\u2019d be an evocative way to deal damage to Garou). So we may either want to give the fomori additional powers (like Mega Attributes or Unnatural Strength, or do the traditional thing and just outfit them with weapons (probably also including silver).<br /> <br /> I don\u2019t want these fomori to go one on one with a Garou. Just be an interesting opponent that could provide some trouble. Bad dice rolls can make many fights perilous, so even weaker fomori can still be dangerous to Garou. But you may have other ideas.<br /> <br /> So how does someone become this kind of fomori? It obviously cannot be limited to the time of Thrassus\u2019s release in the Apocalypse or being physically near Exhibit # 338 (like how the liquid in Prince of Darkness does). We want a fomori that can be found and fought in our chronicles. Instead, I think small portions of the green liquid must somehow materialize far away and at any time. Why? Because the laws of physics don\u2019t apply in either space or time for Thrassus. That Thrassus will one day be released in the future at some place (or was on Earth after it first arrived long ago) means its liquid form CAN appear <i>anytime</i> and <i>anyplace</i>, even in the past (or future). Maybe this is done through the Warp Reality charm by its special spirit servitors if the STs want something that makes sense to them or explained to the players, and perhaps it is done only under certain rites by the cult.<br /> <br /> Anyway, human becomes in contact with the Thrassus liquid, and it taints them leading to bane possession and transforming into this fomori. This is probably not random, but part of cultist activities. Perform certain rites and the green liquid of Thrassus manifests. They would make good protectors of the local cults once the Garou do discover them and send in a pack to wipe them out. Do we need a new bane to be possessing these fomori? Or just use an existing one - like the new bane with the Warp Reality charm?<br /> <br /> I've come pretty far laying out some ideas for a faction based around Thrassus. So if people would like the thread to continue, it'd be good to hear some commentary and new ideas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-27T07:16:54-07:00","id":1523208,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1522330#post1522330\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is something I think resonates with the savage horror of Werewolf: the Apocalypse. But they can\u2019t be like orcs in a fantasy world. The World of Darkness still needs to be similar in appearance to our own. Pig monsters can\u2019t be showing up in downtown Chicago or besieging a small town. So I would say the Swine Things are not inhabitants of our world. But extra-dimensional alien invaders from outside reality are good. They are attracted to the \u201cpsychic energy\u201d of humanity. They feed on that, not material flesh and blood.</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally, I kind of like the idea of fighting actual, physical pig monsters. It not only reminds me of Darkest Dungeon, as stated before, but also of <a href=\"https://weirdwest.fandom.com/wiki/Weird_West_Wiki\" target=\"_blank\">Weird West</a>. In that game, <a href=\"https://weirdwest.fandom.com/wiki/Pigman\" target=\"_blank\">pigmen</a> are creatures made from adding the brains and hearts of humans to a body of stitched up pig parts, and then animated in a dark ritual. (The feeding of souls to a tree is also involved).<br /> <br /> I'm imagining Wyrmish ritual practices engaged in by low-class Thrassus cults among slughterhouse/meat-packing workers, pig farmers, or butchers. Folks with plenty of access to whole or partial pig carcasses, and have frequent contact with all manner of blood and death, on an industrial scale. At least in modern times, of course. You could easily have this kind of &quot;make pig monster&quot; cults popping up at any point in history where hogs are butchered in abundance.<br /> <br /> The cult stitches together the form of a creature out of pig bits. Often humanoid, though it's hardly limited to that. (You could go WILD with the swine horrors, and it would be appropriate for the more splatterpunk aspects of WtA.). These are then implanted with lesser Banes associated with Thrassus, as an animating force. A kind of primitive form of artificial Fomori, developed ad hoc long before the advent of genetic engineering. Pentex in modern times considers such handiwork terribly crude.<br /> <br /> But the pig-stitcher cult doesn't care. &quot;It's the way grand-daddy did it, and that's good enough for me&quot;.<br /> <br /> What do you do with frankenstein pig monsters? I mean aside from brute labor and defense, of course. I have a few thoughts.<br /> <br /> One aspect of the pigmen from Weird West is that they're hollow shells tied to people ritually sacrificed. In game, it's possible to free the souls put in the tree (as one of the playable PCs had done, being an intelligent pigman), so they come to inhabit the bodies. For our cult, we can perhaps suppose the Bane spirits, through dreams, promise a way to cheat death, or to bring one's loved ones back to life. Craft a surrogate body, and through ritual draw the soul of the departed to inhabit it. Is it really the soul of the deceased being brought back, or just a Bane masquerading as such? It doesn't matter that much, for our purposes. Although I imagine a person who returns in a Bane-riddled pig-Fomori body may go insane before long, assuming they weren't insane to begin with. I do like the idea of a cult harboring half-mad swine-men, the elders of the cult, persisting for centuries. (Indeed, that's VERY Lovecraft).<br /> <br /> (Also imagine them returning the souls of dangerous individuals, like serial killers, for use as agents. Maybe the Bane grants the pig Fomori powers to walk unseen among mortals, or appear less like swine stitched into the rough shape of men. Allowing them to do the cult's work out in the world.)<br /> <br /> Another reason you might create a Fomori pig construct is as an oracular tool. I'm imagining Lord of the Flies, where the rotting pig head stuck on a stake seems to speak to characters. Imagine the cult having one of their own, a swine head oracle granting predictions or advice. Claiming to speak wisdom or the will of Thrassus.<br /> <br /> (I'm reminded that the pig head from Lord of the Flies was visibly attracting, well, flies. Another insect parallel to add to the Thrassus pile. Come to think of it, it also reminds me a bit of the &quot;Ribcage of Vrghhrh&quot;, a &quot;magic item&quot; from <a href=\"https://bottomlesssarcophagus.blogspot.com/2019/02/the-orkus.html\" target=\"_blank\">this blog post</a>.)<br /> <br /> A more messed up reason for making pig-men is, of course, as depraved sex slaves. Thrassus invades the dreams of those with a particularly depraved mind, and teaches them to make a creature they can then have sex with. We need not dwell overmuch on this one, save that's its very in keeping with the Call of Cthulhu idea of cultists waiting for their eldritch god to return and teach them new ways to sin.<br /> <br /> And naturally, making pig monsters can be fairly useful if you think the apocalypse is nigh, and you need troops to fight the Final Battle. It doesn't matter if the apocalypse isn't nigh (yet), like in earlier historical periods. All that matters is the cult believes they need tons of monsters for what they view as an imminent fight. Perhaps amassing so many pig Fomori is a requirement for releasing Thrassus (or they think so).<br /> <br /> Alternatively, the cult may be playing the long game. Creating pig mutants and then pickling them in large jars. Amassing them slowly over years or generations, so they'll have an army to release when the Final Battle comes. Imagine a series of tunnels beneath a pig farm or in a barn, full of shelves of grotesque pig creatures suspended in chemicals. Break one open, and the creature might awaken and attack whatever's nearest. If the Garou start smashing jars, deliberately or accidentally, they may find themselves overwhelmed by a press of squealing flesh.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-27T08:22:12-07:00","id":1523211,"title":"","content":"One thing to add about the pig Fomori: what, exactly, do the Fomori/Banes WANT?<br /> <br /> We've already gone over what the cultists want and use them for, or at least what they think they're getting out of the deal of crafting them. But what's the Bane's motivation here, aside from vague guesses as to Thrassus's will? What urges propel them to action?<br /> <br /> I'm think three: Gluttony, Sloth, and Waste.<br /> <br /> Gluttony because of the classic association of pigs with eating. To eat to excess is to make a pig of yourself. To eat more than one's allotted share is to hog it all. Like the pigmen from Weird West, bereft of human intelligence, the pig Fomori eat whatever they can get their hands on. Whatever they are allowed to eat. Pigs are clever, so the cults they serve must pay close attention, lest they sneak food they aren't allowed. Venturing from the compound to steal crops, livestock, and people for their private larders. The cleverest may even drag living people back and force them into cages, feeding them enough to fatten them for the slaughter. They learned from the best, it seems.<br /> <br /> Pig Fomori rarely share their meals. They're greedy bastards. Wholesome qualities like sharing or a sense of proportionality are decidedly unWyrmish. They're qualities of law, of morality. When Thrassus returns, they'll be unburdened of such concepts, and taught new ways to consume.<br /> <br /> Sloth because a domesticated pig is the manifestation of a post-hunter-gatherer lifestyle. Humans didn't want to go hunting, like a normal predator, so they invented animal husbandry. Turned the feral boar into livestock, so they can have their bacon at home. This descent into indolence is reciprocal, as the pigs grew fat and lazy from having their meals handed to them in a trough. Just as a pig is fattened for the slaughter, humanity and their world of soft edges and secure systems is being fattened for the slaughter of Thrassus.<br /> <br /> Those pig Fomori used for labor must be whipped into action. Left to their own devices, they would be indolent, save in the act of procuring food. They're just clever enough to cheat, and find ways of achieving goals with minimal effort. But they're ultimately sloppy. Being careful takes effort. They'd rather take what they want, and leave others to clean up the mess. This is often what attracts Garou attention to the cult.<br /> <br /> The virtue of laziness is a gift they relish spreading to everyone else. They want others to wallow and let themselves, and their world, go to seed. The Weaver-artifice that binds their master, like the Weaver systems that bind this modern world, break down when there is no longer the will to maintain them. Perhaps the prerequisite to releasing Thrassus lies in getting the world to stop caring. Stop caring that the seas boil and the skies burn. Stop caring that carefully maintained infrastructure crumbles, and carefully shepherded environments are choked by ecological collapse and evasive species. (Invasive species like the Old World Pig is to North America).<br /> <br /> Waste, because a pig Fomori is nothing but a waste. Waste of a pig life (and probably a human life sacrificed to make it?). Waste of perfectly good food. A waste of time, because who goes to the trouble of sewing together parts of a pig carcass? Waste of a world, that is having the Talons released on it, bit by bit. They love to accumulate waste - garbage, feces, etc. - and horde it like treasure. They'll wallow in their own or others' filth. They'll break things, or people, simply because the destruction of perfectly good things amuses them. If they can't eat it, they'll throw it out, seemingly out of spite. There is no conservation here, no sustainability. They will use up everything - even their own would-be cultist masters - then leave it a ruin, of no use to anyone.<br /> <br /> Only the decomposers will feast, when the pig Fomori are done. (That insect connection again). They have powers related to summoning pests, so they can break down organic materials into rotten sludge and have them eaten by flies. On some level the pig Fomori are alive. But on another level, they're dead meat brought back into animation. They might not be currently rotting, but they carry the memory of being rotting meat, attracting flies. (If the layer of filth on their skins didn't do it). Many are living hives, puking or shitting swarms of flies or maggots that do their bidding.<br /> <br /> The cults that make them are so often the lower classes. As the Powers That Be (whether Capital, Monarchy, or Fascism) hollow out the world, those below become more obsessed with breaking the machine down. (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amnesia:_A_Machine_for_Pigs\" target=\"_blank\">A machine for pigs</a>, you might say). But while others may try to dismantle the machine so a more equitable and sustainable society might be created in its place, the Banes, Thrassus, and the Wyrm just want it all brought down and left to rot. To take the edifices, of society, nature, and humanity, and lay it to waste.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-27T22:51:50-07:00","id":1523258,"title":"","content":"These are some great ideas <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar6156_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6156\" data-vbnamecard=\"6156\">Bluecho</a>. I appreciate your link to these other games and blog, as I\u2019ve never heard of them of before, but they seem to have elements I can steal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523208#post1523208\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally, I kind of like the idea of fighting actual, physical pig monsters. It not only reminds me of Darkest Dungeon, as stated before, but also of <a href=\"https://weirdwest.fandom.com/wiki/Weird_West_Wiki\" target=\"_blank\">Weird West</a>.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am often wary of including too many fantastic elements in the World of Darkness because ultimately it\u2019s too easy for the setting to become obviously fantastic and impossible for ordinary people (the vast majority of NPCs in the game world) to ignore it, and thus the WoD is no longer our world through a mirror darkly, but some fantasy world where people need to account for all these fantastic monsters and creatures. But I think you sold me on physical swine things being OK.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523208#post1523208\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In that game, <a href=\"https://weirdwest.fandom.com/wiki/Pigman\" target=\"_blank\">pigmen</a> are creatures made from adding the brains and hearts of humans to a body of stitched up pig parts, and then animated in a dark ritual. (The feeding of souls to a tree is also involved).</div> </div> </div> </div>I would make a appearance distinction between the swine-like spirits of the Gnostic Marauders, and the pigmen/swine things of the physical world because I think its essential the Marauders are seen as not-Gaian in any way. The swine fomori can be obviously pigs. But the Gnostic Marauders should only be swine-like, but more alien like using <a href=\"https://non-aliencreatures.fandom.com/wiki/Swine_Thing\" target=\"_blank\">Wayne Barlowe's depiction of the swine things</a> in his Guide to Fantasy,<br /> <br /> However, I am fine with actual swine somehow being a correspondence to the Gnostic Marauders simply because there are similarities. That\u2019s a lot of how magic works - if you think there is some kind of correspondence, then it exists. And if that still doesn\u2019t seem right to you, well, Thrassus corrupts things anyway so that\u2019s just how it works out. Swine are considered to be unclean by some religions, so that is also an element that can work for us.<br /> <br /> What I would do is have the \u201cpig men\u201d or swine things be is a type of animal fomori. These are based on the swine, not humans. But the magic ritual 1) requires some awful, poisonous concoction fed to the swine (like how Circe turned Odysseus\u2019s crew into swine, and how she transformed Scylla into a monster), and 2) the ritual requires the ritemaster to murder a human and have the swine eat the body. That is what allows the Gnostic Marauder to transform the swine into the fomori. This isn\u2019t a kind of \u201cnatural\u201d bane possession to turn something into a fomori. It\u2019s a particularly peverse and unnatural corruption. But that\u2019s why Thrassus is so dangerous.<br /> <br /> The swine in question are not limited to the domestic pig. But any kind of swine from both the Suidae (Pigs) and Tayassuidae (Peccaries). I\u2019ll also throw in the Tapiridae (Tapirs) because what is important is that they have a pig-like appearance reminiscent of the Marauders. Their actual biology is unimportant.<br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523208#post1523208\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another reason you might create a Fomori pig construct is as an oracular tool. I'm imagining Lord of the Flies, where the rotting pig head stuck on a stake seems to speak to characters. Imagine the cult having one of their own, a swine head oracle granting predictions or advice. Claiming to speak wisdom or the will of Thrassus.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like the idea of severed swine heads, infested with flies and insects, somehow providing oracular guidance due to black magic. It\u2019s evocative. Maybe such heads do not act only as oracles, but as warning beacons and sentries wherever they are placed, so whatever it is they observe (like Garou packs coming to get them) they automatically and telepathically warn the high priest of the cult.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523208#post1523208\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Come to think of it, it also reminds me a bit of the &quot;Ribcage of Vrghhrh&quot;, a &quot;magic item&quot; from <a href=\"https://bottomlesssarcophagus.blogspot.com/2019/02/the-orkus.html\" target=\"_blank\">this blog post</a>.</div> </div> </div> </div>I really like that blog post on \u201cOrkus\u201d. Its interpretation of the classic D&amp;D villain has lots of things we could steal for Thrassus. This quote from that blogpost is very apt:<br /> <br /> <i>The Orkus is physical, mental, social decay. It is the maggots in the carcass. Corruption. Bloat. A stagnant body crumbling under its swollen weight. it is the mind beset by so much misleading, misfortune, terror and inner disgust that it becomes a half-mind. A rot-mind, capable of nothing but screaming in the dark.</i><br /> <br /> And this is a good description of the green liquid inside Exhibit #338:<br /> <br /> <i>The most accurate depiction or manifestation of the Orkus would be a vast lake of black, grimy, foul oil-like sludge, completely putrified. </i><br /> <br /> And this sounds like a good basis for the Thrassus cult:<br /> <br /> <i>Who worships the Orkus? No one. However, you can lose yourself to the Orkus. As a person, but also as a community, or even as a civilisation. It starts with neglect. Of friends, of hygiene, of morals, of duty, of anything that needs care and maintains a structure. This devolves into a fundamental apathy and putrification of the self. At this point all that drives your actions is pure spite, towards everything that you've neglected, because you hate that other people enjoy what you've failed to keep. You must bring them down to your level, because you've lost all hope to pull yourself up again. They must become like you, because the reality of the alternative is too painful for you to bear. So you become a festering bloat that befouls everything, and heals nothing.</i><br /> <br /> I don\u2019t think I\u2019ll tie Thrassus into the undead like the traditional association of Orcus. Bringing in the undead, especially with vampires, creates all kind of association issues with existing WoD lore. Plus we already have the disgusting Spawn of Nyogtha/Tsathoggua thing going that easily replaces that and very tuned into similar body horror.<br /> <br /> <i>Necromancers who draw their power from the Orkus are misanthropic, unhygienic, amoral shit sacks that only resemble people if they are lucky. They'll be infected with the Orkus over time, as they gurgle it up from their innards. Teeth rotting, skin turning black and yellow and gangrenous. Bloat. Necrosis. Their name will turn from Johan into Jhhng into hoarse hiss. They neither sleep nor eat, at first because they neglect doing it, then because they lose the organs to.</i><br /> <br /> Just change the mention of necromancers to simply sorcerers. Voila.<br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523208#post1523208\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And naturally, making pig monsters can be fairly useful if you think the apocalypse is nigh, and you need troops to fight the Final Battle.<br /> ...<br /> Alternatively, the cult may be playing the long game. Creating pig mutants and then pickling them in large jars. Amassing them slowly over years or generations, so they'll have an army to release when the Final Battle comes. Imagine a series of tunnels beneath a pig farm or in a barn, full of shelves of grotesque pig creatures suspended in chemicals. Break one open, and the creature might awaken and attack whatever's nearest. If the Garou start smashing jars, deliberately or accidentally, they may find themselves overwhelmed by a press of squealing flesh.</div> </div> </div> </div>I also like the idea of some cults building deep underground pits where they keep them in pickling jars/containers so that Thrassus will have some kind of army whent he Apocalypse agains. These might be honeycombed all over the earth. Finding these pickling stores and destroying them thus becomes a game objective. And individual swine things can still be encountered as guardians or sentries at places, but not necessarily be overused.<br /> <br /> And of course, these swine things must have a Delerium of their own. But maybe in some people, their Delerium eventually helps breaks down their mind so they now become susceptible to the dream-pull of Thrassus whereas before they were not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-27T22:53:16-07:00","id":1523259,"title":"","content":"I would then tie this into the Skull Pigs. Originally Skull Pigs were not related to the Grondr at all. They were corruption of tapirs and peccaries (or some unknown speces of such) of pre-human North America. I always liked that and disliked when later writers hijacked them into the Grondr simply because they had \u201cpig\u201d in the name. Thus a monsters based in North American pre-history suddenly became an Old World monster. But here, we can use this strange bane of Thrassus as the responsible agent for creation of the Skull Pigs (and the Voodoo Pigs). And we might even use Voodoo Pigs as cult leaders of some of the Thrassus cults. Once magic using pig monsters have an Intelligence of 3, 4, or 5, even humans might begin listening to them.<br /> <br /> How were these Skull Pigs first created? Well we know from Ways of the Wolf that before the Pure Ones arrived that North America was a \u201chellhole of corruption under the Wyrm\u2019s influence\u201d and that the Gamor were its greatest servants. So perhaps the Gamor created them either because they serve Thrassus, or they just utilized it as they tried to utilize a lot of the other Wyrm creatures and minions. So perhaps we can tie the Gamor - or at least individual members of them - into Thrassus as well. Or switch them out for the Vhujunka or something else.<br /> <br /> Then we could use the strange connection Thrassus now has with pigs and tie them into the Grondr and their destruction. The fact that Grondr were supposed to be the cleansers of Gaia, it makes them uniquely suited to clean corruption of Thrassus in the world. Maybe seeing some kind of corrupt pig creature scared some Garou into thinking the Grondr were becoming corrupted. Or perhaps some Grondr had become corrupted just like what would one day happen with the White Howlers.<br /> <br /> If the Grondr were cleansers, then it makes sense a perverted Wyrm corrupted version of them by Thrassus would tie them into pure decomposition and corruption.<br /> <br /> This could lead into some ties for Garou packs of Boar in an angry Okkoto the boar king and \u201cNago was beautiful and wise\u201d kind of way, like how the boar spirit became corrupt in Princess Mononoke.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/sM4nds0_Ke8\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/sM4nds0_Ke8\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/sM4nds0_Ke8\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.youtube.com/shorts/sM4nds0_Ke8 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RKXsnbQt5M4\" data-code=\"RKXsnbQt5M4\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/RKXsnbQt5M4/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> The Boar Incarna might harbor a lot of anger towards Thrassus, and his packs see their war against it as even more of a holy war than usual. Some kind of responsibility of the destruction of the Grondr by Thrassus might explain why the Boar totem does not seem to hold any kind of resentment towards the Garou.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-28T01:38:24-07:00","id":1523264,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523258#post1523258\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am often wary of including too many fantastic elements in the World of Darkness because ultimately it\u2019s too easy for the setting to become obviously fantastic and impossible for ordinary people (the vast majority of NPCs in the game world) to ignore it, and thus the WoD is no longer our world through a mirror darkly, but some fantasy world where people need to account for all these fantastic monsters and creatures. But I think you sold me on physical swine things being OK.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And of course, these swine things must have a Delerium of their own. But maybe in some people, their Delerium eventually helps breaks down their mind so they now become susceptible to the dream-pull of Thrassus whereas before they were not. </div> </div> </div>My thinking with the physical pig Formori was that it wouldn't be all that strange, given the normal ground state of Fomori. They're deformed body horror monsters, the likes our Furry Blender protagonists mow down by the dozens. They're already operating on the razor's edge of blowing the &quot;Masquerade&quot; wide open, just by existing. As such, they either need to be very careful, or have powerful figures (Pentex, the Technocracy, the Camarilla, even just corrupt local cops working for the cult) working in the background to cover them up. It makes sense for the more splatterpunk mode Werewolf often occupies, more than other game lines.<br /> <br /> (Moreover, the secrecy angle gets more easy to handwave if pig Formori are encountered in pre-modern times. When things like monsters are easier to hide in the dark corners of the earth, or even in the wilderness outside villages. These cults, I think, make for great antagonists in these time periods, where the big conspiracies are absent but not needed, because everyone is more superstitious and information travels slower.)<br /> <br /> I sort of tried giving them cover, by saying some pig Fomori might have a Bane power that lets them escape notice or appear less fantastic. But just giving them native Delirium is also a very elegant solution. Befitting of some of the &quot;special&quot; Fomori associated with a Talon of the Wyrm. They don't enforce Delirium through deep-seated psychic trauma written in humanity's DNA, as is the case with Garou, but rather because they are somehow especially wrong on a spiritual level.<br /> <br /> That same wrongness is Lovecraftian in texture. Causing mortals to reject them from their conscious minds, or eroding their sanity. Or perhaps decaying it, to go along with the Orkus theme.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I like the idea of severed swine heads, infested with flies and insects, somehow providing oracular guidance due to black magic. It\u2019s evocative. Maybe such heads do not act only as oracles, but as warning beacons and sentries wherever they are placed, so whatever it is they observe (like Garou packs coming to get them) they automatically and telepathically warn the high priest of the cult. </div> </div> </div>Another angle I just now thought of is for the severed pig heads to be able to mutter incantations. These could either be used to &quot;cast spells&quot;, or the words themselves act as a memetic hazard, causing psychic harm or madness. Again, that erosion/decay of sanity. As if the head is acting as an emissary of Thrassus (or close enough), uttering foul words in a blasphemous tongue from the dawn of human history. Fraying thoughts, spoiling dreams, and putrefying emotions.<br /> <br /> These heads could be mounted on stationary locations, like walls or posts, to act as further defense mechanisms. These would be more interior heads, farther in the bowels of cult territory, while the &quot;more mundane&quot; severed pig heads mounted on periphery locations act as sentries and beacons, as described above. Would-be Garou invaders might become so used to seeing random pig heads hanging around, they don't think to see the ones farther in as tangible, direct threats.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I really like that blog post on \u201cOrkus\u201d. Its interpretation of the classic D&amp;D villain has lots of things we could steal for Thrassus.<br /> ... </div> </div> </div>I'm glad I shared it. The Bottomless Sarcophagus is a great blog, and I'm sad the author has largely moved on to other projects. Still, great body of work to pull from. (I still kind of want to run a Thawing Lands campaign some time.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I also like the idea of some cults building deep underground pits where they keep them in pickling jars/containers so that Thrassus will have some kind of army whent he Apocalypse agains. These might be honeycombed all over the earth. Finding these pickling stores and destroying them thus becomes a game objective. And individual swine things can still be encountered as guardians or sentries at places, but not necessarily be overused. </div> </div> </div>One thing I kind of wanted to emphasize with these &quot;pig-stitcher&quot; cults is that they're kind of designed to break down. It's thematically appropriate, given our talk of &quot;The Orkus&quot;. But importantly, the effects of the cult's activities outlive their founders.<br /> <br /> Sure, random people with ready access to swine parts and of susceptible mindset can be made into cultists through their dreams. They can pop up anywhere. But the pig Formori can often outlive the ones who created them, or even keep being made generation on generation. The idea that cult founders might be reanimated in pig-man bodies gives the cult's essential malice a perverse longevity. Even, of course, as those old wizards slowly degrade - decay - in sanity as the years go by. The nuances of their personality being worn away, until only undying contempt for life and a lust for power remains.<br /> <br /> This process of making pig-slaves need not be limited to human participants. I can't imagine pig Fomori can't, in turn, stitch their own swine progeny. They need to source more pigs, or have dupes to provide them, but they could keep doing it. To paraphrase the lore on the Necromancers from Darkest Dungeon: &quot;the pigs reanimating the pigs, forever&quot;.<br /> <br /> (I'm also reminded of the <a href=\"https://mtg.fandom.com/wiki/Thrull\" target=\"_blank\">Thrulls</a> from Magic: The Gathering, from the plane of Dominaria. In MtG, Thrulls are constructs made from dead flesh. But while on the plane of Ravnica the creators of Thrulls, the Orzhov Syndicate, continue to make such servants as they've done for 10,000 years uninterrupted, on Dominaria the Thrulls overtook their masters. On the Dominarian continent of Sarpadia, the Order of the Ebon Hand created Thrulls as slaves and sacrifice fodder. But gradually started using them as battle constructs, and even as assistants for rituals. Once the Thrulls learned how to make more Thrulls, it was over for the Ebon Hand. They came to eradicate almost all other people on the continent, and rule it to this day. It's said to be a blessing for Dominaria that the Thrulls aren't aware anywhere else but Sarpadia exists. Even <a href=\"https://mtg.fandom.com/wiki/Phyrexian_Invasion_of_Dominaria\" target=\"_blank\">Phyrexians</a> failed to conquer it.)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-28T12:36:34-07:00","id":1523284,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523264#post1523264\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Moreover, the secrecy angle gets more easy to handwave if pig Formori are encountered in pre-modern times.</div> </div> </div> </div>Definitely true. One issue I have with published WtA is that so many of its antagonists are things that can only appear recently as a result of technological progress. But you can't have PENTEX operating in the Middle Ages, and even old standbys like the Black Spiral Dancers shouldn't be a threat until post-WWII and didn't exist until the Roman Empire. But the Garou have been fighting the Wyrm for thousands of years before it. None of the Dark Ages supplements ever gave us good replacements for them. And if someone wanted to run a game during the Roman Republic, Iron Age Germany, the Bronze Age Britain, etc., they'd almost have to build everything from scratch. When creating my own Wyrm factions, I like to put in commentary like &quot;this group was a mighty foe during the Neolithic, but the Garou destroyed them and now only few such beings remain&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523264#post1523264\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another angle I just now thought of is for the severed pig heads to be able to mutter incantations.</div> </div> </div> </div>That certainly has potential. For myself, I'd probably make this more like the Gift Mindspeak. It's less noticeable - helping my obsession with trying to keep monsters secret - and the waking dream aspect already ties in nicely with the dream-pull of Thrassus. So the cult members can understand, but ordinary people just see rotting pig heads. Of course, spiritually advanced people might hear something like whispering that seems to be coming from the pig heads, or even more detailed mind speech. For Garou, I'd probably use Gnosis as a marker. If you have Gnosis at 5 or above, you overhear perfectly. At 1 Gnosis, you just hear what you think is someone whispering, and it gradually increases as you have more Gnosis. But you can always spend 1 point of temporary Gnosis to overhear a scene. Except Metis of course, they ALWAYS hear everything perfectly regardless of how much Gnosis they have.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523264#post1523264\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But importantly, the effects of the cult's activities outlive their founders.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like this. Thrauss cults occasionally do things - like create swine things - before collapsing or being wiped out, but they still exist as little time bombs that can go off. That provides more variety for an ST on how to introduce some thing. Maybe some swine things awake from their pickle jars and begin ravaging an area, but the local Garou sept don't initially know what is going on because the last time they fought (and destroyed) a local cult of Thrassus was back in Colonial times or the Middle Ages or the time of the Mound Builders or whatever. Then at some point maybe ask one of the Galliards (NPC or PC as appropriate) to make a roll to see what old legend they might remember learning.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523264#post1523264\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The idea that cult founders might be reanimated in pig-man bodies gives the cult's essential malice a perverse longevity.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like this. While in my version, the pigs aren't stitched together, but become fomori after imbibing some foul concoction and then eating a human body, those bodies might not be from someone they kidnapped and killed. Maybe some of the Thrassus cults who make these fomori, simply have their own bodies consumed by the corrupted swine after they die on their own (either naturally or by violence). Because they think the swine fomori who ate their body somehow contains their &quot;essence&quot;, perhaps &quot;knowledge&quot; or something else. Some cultists willingly allowing their bodies to be eaten (usually after they are dead, but perhaps some deranged souls do it while they are alive) by swine would be a very disturbing image to share with PCs.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523264#post1523264\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can't imagine pig Fomori can't, in turn, stitch their own swine progeny. They need to source more pigs, or have dupes to provide them, but they could keep doing it. To paraphrase the lore on the Necromancers from Darkest Dungeon: &quot;the pigs reanimating the pigs, forever&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>For my own chronicles, I would probably limit this as something that would only begin when the Apocalypse is near (which could, of course, happen in modern games when the ST wants the setting to imply the countdown to the Apocalypse has begun). Simply because, again, I'm nervous about too many monsters being too noticeable. But it could be something I reference as various Garou with the Gift Sight From Beyond have - pig men making pig men. They don't seem to be doing that now... but if theurges keep having dreams about that, then at some point you know it is going to happen. Probably. But when?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-28T12:50:50-07:00","id":1523287,"title":"","content":"Other things that the swine aspect of the cult of Thrassus, is that we could tie in some mythological and folkloric references.<br /> <br /> Myth has all sorts of dangerous boar (or boar like creatures) that the legendary heroes had to fight. Theseus slew the Crommyonian Sow. Hercules had to capture the Erymanthian Boar as one of this Labors. A group of heroes including Atalanta and Meleager, hunted the Calydonian boar. The knights of King Arthur hunted Twrch Trwyth and Ysgtithyrwyn. Were some of these dangerous fomori associated with Thrassus? Even if not, we can place similar monstrous boars as such.<br /> <br /> Then there are 17th century legends of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pig-faced_women\" target=\"_blank\">Pig-faced women</a>. Are they the result of a certain Thrassus cult becoming noteworthy enough that it impacted folklore of western Europe during this time? Or the appearances of &quot;<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Learned_pig\" target=\"_blank\">learned pigs</a>&quot; in the 18th century that could answer people's questions by picking out the appropriate word cards laid before it. Might be ways for certain swine things to go out in public, and introduces the possibility of carnivals and freak shows being ways the cult moves around. Garou PCs might be surprised the source of some local evil is the &quot;pig&quot; held by the travelling carnival that appeared in the area recently.<br /> <br /> I don't want to associate Thrauss too much with the swine theme. There should be other, stronger references. But combined with others and used appropriately, I think it can be useful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-28T17:12:42-07:00","id":1523294,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523284#post1523284\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Definitely true. One issue I have with published WtA is that so many of its antagonists are things that can only appear recently as a result of technological progress. But you can't have PENTEX operating in the Middle Ages, and even old standbys like the Black Spiral Dancers shouldn't be a threat until post-WWII and didn't exist until the Roman Empire. But the Garou have been fighting the Wyrm for thousands of years before it. None of the Dark Ages supplements ever gave us good replacements for them. And if someone wanted to run a game during the Roman Republic, Iron Age Germany, the Bronze Age Britain, etc., they'd almost have to build everything from scratch. When creating my own Wyrm factions, I like to put in commentary like &quot;this group was a mighty foe during the Neolithic, but the Garou destroyed them and now only few such beings remain&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>Definitely. One of the essential aspects of Werewofl, versus the other games, is how <i>primal</i> it all is. The Garou are creatures of spirit, born at the beginning of the world. And the Wyrm is no new threat, but a slow-building rot at the center of reality. It's a problem that should fester throughout history, without the aid of modern technology.<br /> <br /> Having Wyrmish antagonists independent of the present-day paradigm is important. Both for supplying &quot;period-piece&quot; chronicles with bad guys, but also to give more problems for today's Garou. Even when Pentex or the Black Spiral Dancers are around, they don't need to be the only ones engaging in Wyrmish evil.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I like this. Thrauss cults occasionally do things - like create swine things - before collapsing or being wiped out, but they still exist as little time bombs that can go off. That provides more variety for an ST on how to introduce some thing. Maybe some swine things awake from their pickle jars and begin ravaging an area, but the local Garou sept don't initially know what is going on because the last time they fought (and destroyed) a local cult of Thrassus was back in Colonial times or the Middle Ages or the time of the Mound Builders or whatever. Then at some point maybe ask one of the Galliards (NPC or PC as appropriate) to make a roll to see what old legend they might remember learning. </div> </div> </div>All manner of &quot;ticking time bombs&quot; can be set up with Thrassus cults. Swing things in jars, left buried for centuries (or millennia).<br /> <br /> Particularly foul pig Fomori wizards, locked in a sarcophagus in ancient times by superstitious locals. Festering in a pool of their own rot. (It reminds me of that black sarcophagus found a while back, filled with urine and feces by the ones who buried them.) In this case, the rot is a product of the Bane's influence. A kind of microcosm of Thrassus itself, with tiny microfractures causing the sarcophagus to leak and corrupt/influence those who come into contact with it. Maybe some archaeologists find it at a dig, and the university/museum it's stored in becomes the nexus for a depraved cult (possibly under the control of the wizard).<br /> <br /> Some proto-scientist or physician might have written plans on how to stitch together or develop a swine Fomori, as part of their descent into madness. They died, but their plans weren't destroyed. (I'm reminded of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embalming_(manga)\" target=\"_blank\"><i>Embalming: The Another Tale of Frankenstein</i></a> [sic]. A manga about an alternate history where Frankenstein's notes survived in two volumes, which were copied and passed around in secret. Each evil or insane scientist iterating on the base principles, until there was a global reanimation arms-race. Basically imagine that, but explicitly Wyrm-tained. EDIT: Actually, I should warn away from giving this series too much attention. I just learned it was made by <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobuhiro_Watsuki\" target=\"_blank\">Nobuhiro Watsuki,</a> creator of Rurouni Kenshin and guy who was busted for child p*rn possession. Don't give this guy money, if you have the opportunity to do so.)<br /> <br /> Perhaps even ancient nursery rhymes or legends carry the seed of Thrassus. Those susceptible becomes obsessed with this lingering artifact of cultural memory, a tainted meme that inspires dreams of Thrassus.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I like this. While in my version, the pigs aren't stitched together, but become fomori after imbibing some foul concoction and then eating a human body, those bodies might not be from someone they kidnapped and killed. Maybe some of the Thrassus cults who make these fomori, simply have their own bodies consumed by the corrupted swine after they die on their own (either naturally or by violence). Because they think the swine fomori who ate their body somehow contains their &quot;essence&quot;, perhaps &quot;knowledge&quot; or something else. Some cultists willingly allowing their bodies to be eaten (usually after they are dead, but perhaps some deranged souls do it while they are alive) by swine would be a very disturbing image to share with PCs. </div> </div> </div>I do think there's room in the game for both &quot;stitched&quot; and &quot;fed&quot; swine Fomori. And there's advantages to both, under different situations.<br /> <br /> &quot;Fed&quot; swine things are arguably easier and more primitive. Mix the right (or wrong) concoction, feed it to a pig, along with all manner of human parts. It's something that can be done on a farm, with one's own livestock. Indeed, it might be done <i>accidently</i>. Maybe the farmers feed them a concoction, they thought of in a dream, thinking it will help the pig. Or maybe a murder happens, and they try to dispose of the body by feeding it to the pigs. The weight of the transgression attracts the Bane. What's worse, if the farmers butcher the pigs and eat them, they get tainted too. And not just by Prion Disease that will make swiss cheese of their brains in thirty years. Wyrm taint, that makes them susceptible to Thrassus. A kind of self-reinforcing feedback loop of corruption that makes monsters of livestock and farmer both.<br /> <br /> The advantage of &quot;stitched&quot; swine Fomori is you don't need as much access to live pigs, only pig parts. For modern times, this makes it a favored &quot;project&quot; by meat-packers, who have access to all manner of bits. (Even human bits. In the sort of Upton Sinclair &quot;The Jungle&quot; days of early industrial meat-packing, safety standards are all a mess. Workers may randomly find a human hand lopped off with one of their colleagues had an accident. Does the person need to be sacrificed to make a pig Fomori? Or is merely the transgression of mixing pig and &quot;long pig&quot; together enough? Something to consider.)<br /> <br /> In pre-modern times, &quot;stitched&quot; pig Fomori are rarer, though could still be found among butchers or aspiring surgeons. (Can't always dissect a human to learn Anatomy. Might be forced to dissect pigs to learn how the body works. No one said when you were done taking it apart, you couldn't put it back together in new shapes.) It requires a greater degree of sewing skill, and a basic grasp of how meat fits together, so it's generally out of the reach of most pre-modern cultures. Aside from the rare rural enthusiast, I'd imagine. The dreams of Thrassus might provide the &quot;spackle&quot; needed to fill in gaps in a layman's knowledge of anatomy. (Isn't that a horrifying mental image? A random villager is beset by dreams of meat, until they start seeing how they connect. Then they start getting ideas...)<br /> <br /> One place you might find pig-stitchers is among military surgeons. An army on campaign needs men to saw off gangrenous limbs or pull out arrows/crossbow bolts/bullets. And those same armies need a plurality of livestock on hand to feed the men. If a few parts end up in the surgeon's tent instead of the cooking pot, would anyone notice? Moreover, the horrors of war can leave folk in such a mental state as to be susceptible to supernatural influence. Especially if they're the ones sawing off limbs or trying to sew men's guts back into their bellies. Seeing the rot set in on a soldier's leg. Pits filled with filth and blood and bodies.<br /> <br /> Truly, it's enough to make a man sick. Sick in the head, too. Make a whole company sick. Thrassus cults might form under such conditions. Imagine Garou fighting mercenaries during the Napoleonic wars, having lopped off limbs in a vain attempt to stop the rot, only for it to settle in their bones and minds. When the ammunition runs out, they call on the surgeon to bring forth the <i>pig-thing</i>. Or lead the Garou into an open pit of putrescent black ichor, the remains of a former mass grave where Thrassus's influence was permitted to accumulate and fester.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For my own chronicles, I would probably limit this as something that would only begin when the Apocalypse is near (which could, of course, happen in modern games when the ST wants the setting to imply the countdown to the Apocalypse has begun). Simply because, again, I'm nervous about too many monsters being too noticeable. But it could be something I reference as various Garou with the Gift Sight From Beyond have - pig men making pig men. They don't seem to be doing that now... but if theurges keep having dreams about that, then at some point you know it is going to happen. Probably. But when? </div> </div> </div>I can definitely see the logic in making self-perpetuating pig cults a sign of the coming Apocalypse.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, I like the idea it might have happened a few times before in history, albeit rarely. The kinds of signs Garou storytellers would speak of, to provide clues to the party that matters are getting very bad.<br /> <br /> Moreover, just because the Apocalypse is &quot;supposed&quot; to happen in modern day, doesn't mean it can't threaten to happen in &quot;period-piece&quot; chronicles. There's always a chance, in a chronicle set in the past, that the Apocalypse might come early if the PCs don't sort things out fast. When the stars are right, and when conditions for Thrassus's release threaten to come to pass, the pigs may begin making pigs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":6156,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","user":"Bluecho","date":". 09-28-2023, 09:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-04T14:38:14-07:00","id":1523573,"title":"","content":"I think it is important to emphasize horror in any faction of the Wyrm. This is not a game of heroic fantasy, but savage horror. It is very easy for STs and PCs to reduce the bad guys into disposable goons so as ST I try to remember to portray the minions of the Wyrms in a creepy or dreadful way. That doesn\u2019t mean it needs to be a tough combat fight, or that the Garou PCs themselves are scared. After all, Garou PCs must be able to handle an ongoing war with such creatures. But if you can creep the players out, it\u2019d worth it.<br /> <br /> Something I find very similar to the body horror of beings like Knygathin Zhaum is Junji Ito\u2019s horror character Tomie. For those who don\u2019t know, Tomie is somethin akin to a succubus, enciting deep lust and attraction in men and even able to control them. She leads them into some kind of psychological breakdown and acts of violence. Now that aspect has nothing to do with Thrassus. However, the tie into this kind of fomori is that whenever she is killed, she is able to regenerate (or even replicate) herself, effectively making her immortal. Furthermore, she is even able to replicate herself by taking over other victim\u2019s bodies. People who receive an organ transplant or blood donation from her transform into her. Locks of her hair can burrow into people\u2019s brains possessing them, later killing them as the hair grows wildly within their bodies.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AJoZrBy2RR0\" data-code=\"AJoZrBy2RR0\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/AJoZrBy2RR0/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> That kind of plasticity and body horror ties in well with our themes, and the bizarre and horrific survivability is perfect for this type of fomori (it might be one of the less powerful kind, but probably more likely one of the Ferectoi if our intention is to develop a particular NPC as opposed to just having something weird happen). The corruption of the flesh as a result of the green liquid infects as well as destroys. In WtA, these fomori don\u2019t need to be unkillable or always return. Good players should quickly figure out permanent ways to dispose of them. But the bizarre ways they might survive is a great way to play up actual horror and freak out players. We don\u2019t need to duplicate this perfectly, and certainly not for the same fomori NPC. But we can steal various bits of it for a variety of fomori NPCs (perhaps limited to Ferectoi).<br /> <br /> I find it is rarely possible for an ST to scare players when ther PCs are in direct conflict with the monsters. That doesn\u2019t see heroic to them, and they\u2019ll downplay anything the ST does. During other scenes however - when fights aren\u2019t happening - players are more willing to acknowledge any unease or terror they are feeling, and the kind of events above are examples of things that creep people out. After they kill a Ferectoi of Thrassus, having them see someone - perhaps some NPC they already know - slowly turn into that same Ferectoi because they had a blood transfusion or kidney donation that came from the Ferectoi can be very unsettling.<br /> <br /> Thrassus and its minions are just an entropic force in the universe leading to everything breaking down - social relationships, individual psyches, society in general, controlling the natural forces of decomposition in general, and eventually to the breakdown of the laws of biology and physics itself.<br /> <br /> In general, I think the bizarre and unsettling horror works of Junji Ito is perfect for Thrassus with how they showcase a breakdown of the natural world. Of course, things found in stories like Uzumaki, Smashed, Remina, and Gyo can\u2019t be introduced into the game without fundamentally changing the world from one that is supposed to be ignorant of the supernatural. But I think it\u2019s exactly the kind of thing we might see when the Apocalypse begins and the great forces of supernatural evil rise. So you can steal imagery for use with prophetic Gifts like Sight from Beyond, and you might be able to steal some limited imagery and ideas in some encounters.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7CEl663buL4\" data-code=\"7CEl663buL4\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/7CEl663buL4/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-04T21:03:29-07:00","id":1523592,"title":"","content":"Hollow Men I imagine are strong contenders for &quot;endlessly survivable&quot;. Even if you tear the skin suit to ribbons and crunch the mass of vermin to paste, so long as a single tainted member of the swarm survives, they can slink away and begin again.<br /> <br /> They might assemble a new swarm, then stitch together a new &quot;container&quot; from materials like burlap. (Scarecrows or cloth manikins are useful for this, as they're already human-shaped assemblages of flexible material. Just hop in, eat away the stuffing, and the Hollow Man is good to go). Real <a href=\"https://the-nightmare-before-christmas.fandom.com/wiki/Oogie_Boogie\" target=\"_blank\">Oogie Boogie</a> vibes.<br /> <br /> Alternatively, the swarm finds a wounded person (or someone who can be MADE wounded), and pours into the opening to hollow them out. A dark mirror of the process whereby the Hollow Man was originally created. It certainly matches that scene from Prince of Darkness, where the murdered guy is puppeted by the insects until his body falls apart and is &quot;recycled&quot;. This kind of consumptive possession by swarm furthers the theme of Thrassus's minions being a creeping force of decay. Every vessel is of finite usefulness, before the entropic principle devours them from the inside out.<br /> <br /> If a Hollow Man takes over new bodies, it's likely they take on more and more memories. W20 Book of the Wyrm frames the Fomor almost like the vestiges of the person whose skin the swarm wears. That the swarm is a separate but vital party to their horrific shared existence. If the skin is destroyed and a new body is claimed, whatever personhood that remains might be discarded along with it.<br /> <br /> Or that personhood could follow along with the swarm (hence the framing as a creature that survives from one encounter to another). With the caveat that each new body overlaps the memories and psychological quirks over the chittering, verminous center. The nuances of the Hollow Man's personality becoming drowned out by many vessels, until they slowly lose track of who they are or were. That is, unless there's some powerful personality that dominates.<br /> <br /> (I'm reminded of Hellsing, where the vampire Alucard is not merely Dracula, but every person he ever consumed over the centuries. It's actually a late series plot point that any single soul devoured into Alucard has no sense of self. Their personhood drowned in a sea of other souls, unable to identify a division between them. A Hollow Man using this framework may experience radical character shifts every time they &quot;die&quot; and come back.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-04T22:19:27-07:00","id":1523602,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523592#post1523592\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hollow Men I imagine are strong contenders for &quot;endlessly survivable&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>You made some very trenchant observations about Hollow Men that I'll be using from now on. I think in the beginning, the personality of the new host dominates completely. But as time goes by, the swarm takes over, the new host's personality lessens, and older hosts emerge. Eventually some kind of gestalt personality lead by the Bane emerges.<br /> <br /> The idea of one of the vermin escaping destruction so the monster survives reminds me of a story about a Russian vampire in the Night Creatures volume of the Time-Life Enchanted World series.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure how appropriate the Oogie Boogie Man is to Hollow Man, but obviously his composure of bugs fits. Nice reference though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uwlM46Olwbc\" data-code=\"uwlM46Olwbc\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/uwlM46Olwbc/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-08T18:38:49-07:00","id":1523851,"title":"","content":"I've really been enjoying this thread, sadly I didn't really have the time to gather some of my thoughts and ideas this has provoked.<ul><li>The dreams and corruption make me think of the Illigoir - since we're already going into the Mythos, they are also these strange untouchable presences that rot both people and places from within and are locked into the place they are and need to be handled in roundabout ways</li> <li>The rise of man and the rise of domesticated beasts that exist only for food are a cool vector, considering that animal husbandry is very Weaverish, what is it that broke here and how did the corruption of Husbandry turn into the foundations of the cult?</li> <li>The influence on dreams does make me think of a faction of the Fera that have a lot to do with dreaming - what would the interaction between the Mokole and the servants of Thrassus be? Is it possible that the Unborn and the corruption of Mnesis could be tied into an entity like this one? Is it possible that Mnesis is under constant attack from beings like this? We could even tie in the lack of Mokole ancestors and say that the reason they don't have access to them is because they are fighting to keep Mnesis safe from such corruptive influences?</li> <li>The cult itself feels like the most intriguing element for me, the whole pig monster / pig slaughter thing is graphic and evokes a certain set of visuals. Maybe the people influenced by Thrassus find strange rites to commune with this entity, and the rite involves impaling a pig head on a stake, and stuffing its mouth with feces, rot and blood, and if performed correctly, it speaks and instructs the intiate in more disturbing secrets. The longest surving cult leaders could even have rooms that serve as councils of dead pigs where stench, rot and flies are a small price to pay for the blasphemous wisfom of the Pig Chorus</li> <li>What if there is a tiny crack in the container. Not enough for the whole thing to escape, but enough for droplets to form once in a while, maybe once every year or once every 7 years? The place where these droplets drop is tainted and changed by the urges of Thrassus and cults are spawned where it forms. Actual droplets in a container are one of the most sought after relic for the membership for using them in their rites can achieve the greatest of magic if not even apotheosis as an avatar of Thrassus in the world?</li> <li>A military group of followers of Thrassus who survived horrible wounds only to bear the rot within them and led by the medic willing to do anything to keep them alive would be an intriguing spin on the first team and a viable antagonist group for a pack, that's a really neat idea <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar6156_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6156\" data-vbnamecard=\"6156\">Bluecho</a></li> <li>Love the thoughts on the Hollow Men, and the transfer of conciousness is also a cool idea, one that ties into the Worms that Walk</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-08T19:19:11-07:00","id":1523855,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523851#post1523851\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">[*]What if there is a tiny crack in the container. Not enough for the whole thing to escape, but enough for droplets to form once in a while, maybe once every year or once every 7 years? The place where these droplets drop is tainted and changed by the urges of Thrassus and cults are spawned where it forms. Actual droplets in a container are one of the most sought after relic for the membership for using them in their rites can achieve the greatest of magic if not even apotheosis as an avatar of Thrassus in the world?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it can generally be assumed that there's a tiny leak in the container. Slowly weeping concentrated drops of evil. The presence of Thrassus in the collective unconscious (or The Dreaming? There's an idea worth exploring...) grows a little bit with each drop. A spiritual poison slowly building in the world until, when the time is right, it hits a saturation point and the Talon is released.<br /> <br /> Because of that, even setting aside the drop's potent magical value, it's release into the world in and of itself increases Thrassus's influence. The fact it got out is a problem, let alone what someone can do with it.<br /> <br /> Naturally, we know the container is currently in the possession of organization presumably unaffiliated with Thrassus worship. So the drops won't necessarily reach a follower, except through indirect means.<br /> <br /> However, once I remembered it might be the Technocracy that has the container, a thought occurred: &quot;Would the Technocracy be trying to study the substance?&quot;<br /> <br /> Which in turn led me to the additional thought: &quot;<i>What if the Syndicate's Special Projects Division got their hands on a sample?</i>&quot;<br /> <br /> THAT is a good question. That's a scary question.<br /> <br /> The SPD has traditionally been a massive source of conflict for games related to the Technocracy, to the point during the old Guide to the Technocracy, it was the subject of a whole counter-conspiracy. The SPD is no stranger to working with Wyrm-tainted technology. What sort of damage could they do with a tangible fragment of the Wyrm's physical essence on Earth?<br /> <br /> Granted, at least by later writings, the SPD is no longer a part of the Technocracy. They suddenly picked up shop and disappeared. On the one hand, a game where Thrassus cults are a major antagonist doesn't need to use that development. Most Werewolf games will be minimally privy to the internal politics of the Technocratic Union. The ST could run with the assumption the SPD is still rooted in the Syndicate and therefore Technocracy. Making the Garou pack need even team up with Project Invictus to root the SPD out, to stop the misuse of the drops from the container.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, just because SPD left the Technocracy, doesn't mean they couldn't take one or more drops with them. Leaving the Union with a number of samples of highly volatile material even it cannot account for.<br /> <br /> Is it worse that the SPD could exploit the Thrassus drops with the full resources of the Syndicate at their disposal? Or is it worse that they can exploit them without even tenuous oversight?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-08T23:50:52-07:00","id":1523863,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523851#post1523851\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've really been enjoying this thread, sadly I didn't really have the time to gather some of my thoughts and ideas this has provoked.</div> </div> </div> </div>The thread has gotten a lot of likes and views, but the lac of responses until recently has been frustrating for me. I really appreciate <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar6156_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6156\" data-vbnamecard=\"6156\">Bluecho</a> commenting which has given this thread a second life. Sharing your ideas is one thing. Actual discussion of what works and new ideas in response is far more beneficial.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523851#post1523851\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The rise of man and the rise of domesticated beasts that exist only for food are a cool vector, considering that animal husbandry is very Weaverish, what is it that broke here and how did the corruption of Husbandry turn into the foundations of the cult?</div> </div> </div> </div>I had not given thought that the act of domestication itself might be some vector of the cult. It is an intriguing angle. For my part, I just wanted to include aspects of William Hope Hodgson as I thought they were appropriate, and referenced Darkest Dungeon because I like what they did with the swine-thing horror angle. <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar6156_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6156\" data-vbnamecard=\"6156\">Bluecho</a> added more pig horror reference. There's just some really horrific imagery you can use here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2sYqStl4rQY\" data-code=\"2sYqStl4rQY\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/2sYqStl4rQY/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> But I don't associate it with animal husbandry per se, just the unfortunate coincidence that the swine things of House on the Borderland are reminiscent of actual pigs.<br /> <br /> Still, if something can be done with it, I'm not against it. Is there an association because the act of domestication of wild animals somehow unnatural? Perhaps does Thrassus' influence on humanity somehow reflect a kind of domestication like how humanity domesticates wild animals? It could lead to some useful things.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523851#post1523851\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The influence on dreams does make me think of a faction of the Fera that have a lot to do with dreaming - what would the interaction between the Mokole and the servants of Thrassus be?</div> </div> </div> </div>Dreams, in general, are a neglected aspect of WtA despite several sections of the corebook being devoted to it. I think anything that can make dreams more vital to the game to be a benefit. The Mokole and Mnesis being somehow threatened by Thrassus is an excellent angle to explore.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523851#post1523851\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What if there is a tiny crack in the container. Not enough for the whole thing to escape, but enough for droplets to form once in a while, maybe once every year or once every 7 years?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think having a crack versus not having a crack actually changes thing. The only important thing is the narrative being used in your chronicle and the flavor text able to create a sense of unease in your PCs. If having a crack that allows so much fluid to escape every year, seven years, a century, a millennia, etc. works for your gaming group, then by all means do it. if such a schedule allows you to justify having some horrible event, even better.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523851#post1523851\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Love the thoughts on the Hollow Men, and the transfer of conciousness is also a cool idea, one that ties into the Worms that Walk</div> </div> </div> </div>I recognize that phrase from the Call of Cthulhu scenario Shadows of Yog-Sothoth, as well as the D&amp;D campaign of the Age of Worms. Then again, TVTropes has an entire page on the trope that extends beyond it. So the horror of an terrifyingly powerful creature composed of a colony of vermin is not unique. Should a more powerful type creature be included for the faction around Thrassus? If so, what should be the lore and function and abilities of it?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-09T00:14:54-07:00","id":1523865,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1523855#post1523855\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which in turn led me to the additional thought: &quot;<i>What if the Syndicate's Special Projects Division got their hands on a sample?</i>&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>It would certainly be an interesting aspect of the threat of Thrassus. Instead of serving Thrassus (at least knowingly), you would have an adjacent threat as a result of experiments with a thing mankind was never meant to know. The key would be to utilize aspects of Thrassus that would not normally be developed with the actual cult. It would have to be unique to the SPD. But as some kind of physics/reality breaking force on Earth being investigated by rational materialist-reductionist super-scientists, you could probably do a lot. For some reason, this remind of the books/movie Annihilation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zy5TsNHlSDU\" data-code=\"zy5TsNHlSDU\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/zy5TsNHlSDU/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-15T22:00:16-07:00","id":1524123,"title":"","content":"The concept of the Talons of the Wyrm were introduced in the early 1992 sourcebook Rite of Passage which only gave us Narlthus (and made it clear that the actual status of Narlthus was up to the ST). The next mention of the Talons was in 1993\u2019s 1e Players Guide which mentions in the Black Fury tribal section that they are actively seeking three of the Talons. The Sept of Artemis and Sept of the Black Pegasus each guard one. The third presumably was being searched for. That same year in Caerns of Power, we\u2019re introduced to the Sept of Bygone Visions but no mention of any Talon is made.<br /> <br /> 1993 is also the year of Rage Across Russia, and once again the Talons are mentioned. Koschei is introduced, and thus between Koschei and Narlthus, we know the names of the Talons that the Black Furies are not looking for. But we are given new information - now we are told that \u201canother Talon, whose name has been forgotten, is carefully guarded by the Sept of Bygone Visions, a Black Fury caern in the Mediterreanean Sea. Almost no Garou in the world today knows that it is there, and no one knows which object within the vast horde kept by the sept controls the Talon.\u201d It makes no mentions of the Talons supposedly at the other two Black Fury septs, but implies they may have been forgotten by the world.<br /> <br /> This is very strange since at face value, the information in the Players Guide implies the two Talons guarded by the Septs of Artemis and Black Pegasus are the ones that should be best known. But now it seems they\u2019ve been completely forgotten. Since all three sourcebooks date from 1993, I don\u2019t think there was any retcon, but this (contradictory) information reveal was coordinated. If nothing else, this was something then developer of the line, Bill Bridges, was interested in establishing. But the contradiction between the three sourcebooks is hard to reconcile. The easiest would be too assume that the Furies at those septs know what they are guarding, but everyone has forgotten about them. At the same time, the Furies also captured the third Talon, but subsequently forgot they had it. That works, but I find this a weird situation.<br /> <br /> I don\u2019t think any Talons are mentioned again. Bill Bridges steps down as developer of Werewolf, and Ethan Skemp takes over. It isn\u2019t until 1998 that the Book of the Weaver introduces Exhibit # 338 There we are told \u201ca few, pointing to same-time similarities between this item\u2019s discovery and the recent struggles at the Sept of Bygone Visions, whisper that it serves as prison to no less an entity than Thrassus the Thrice Damned, Smallest Talon of the Wyrm itself.\u201d<br /> <br /> Assuming we don\u2019t simply ignore all this as misinformation, there is an implied backstory here. This Talon at the Sept of Bygone Visions is somehow forgotten. This might be a result of the Black Fury Iona Kinslayer (falsely) telling Garou that all artifacts hidden at the caern had been destroyed back after WWII. But there could be something more nefarious going on.<br /> <br /> We also know there is some dissension at the sept. Other Black Furies seem to be angered by this sept\u2019s leadership. Iona violated ancient bans and opened the sept to outsiders. Another leader, Kyra Firefoot, has a known friendship with a Leech (the Gangrel Brunhilde of all people). There is perhaps an implied backstory that Kyra somehow utilized Brundhilde to stop the Get of Fenris attack at the caern in 1967 although this is never stated and the origin of their \u201cold friendship\u201d is not given. But it makes sense to me that when Kyra was a \u201cvagabond\u201d she somehow befriended Brunhilde after she rose from torpor and Brunhilde has helped her against their common enemy - the Get. There is also a male Metis at the sept, Tireseias. And we know Kyra and Iona squabble. So we have lots of potential reasons why this sept might undergo turmoil from within the sept, between the Black Furies of this sept and other Black Fury septs, and between the Black Furies and outsiders like the Get of Fenris and Shadow Lords.<br /> <br /> Presumably some turmoil did come to a head, at which point the fetish prison of Thrassus was removed by parties unknown. I assume Exhbitit # 338 is now in some vault of the Technocracy in North America, although again an ST can assign this to some other group. The most likely explanation IMO is that Thrassus somehow arranged his escape to a move favorable location. I just cannot imagine a situation where outsiders invaded the sept secretly and took Exhibit # 338 at random. If they took it, it was intentionally. And since the description in Book of the Weaver does not indicate that the people at the storage location know what it is, then it implies any intention was from Exhibit # 338 itself.<br /> <br /> This implies that proximity to Thrassus could result in memory loss and amnesia, as well as ratcheting up social tensions among the people around him. Possibly attracting outsiders (other Garou tribes, the Technocracy, Wyrm minions) to attack the sept overtly or clandestinely via some kind of dream summons or subtle influence. Such memory loss and amnesia can easily be associated with a Delerium effect which we already discussed in regards to Thrassus.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 10-16-2023, 03:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-15T23:09:37-07:00","id":1524125,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524123#post1524123\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This implies that proximity to Thrassus could result in memory loss and amnesia, as well as ratcheting up social tensions among the people around him. Possibly attracting outsiders (other Garou tribes, the Technocracy, Wyrm minions) to attack the sept overtly or clandestinely via some kind of dream summons or subtle influence. Such memory loss and amnesia can easily be associated with a Delerium effect which we already discussed in regards to Thrassus.</div> </div> </div> </div>Tangential to the thread, but the idea of greater Wyrm entities inflicting Delirium on Garou and the Changing Breeds is a neat and flavorful one.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-15T23:19:20-07:00","id":1524126,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524123#post1524123\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This implies that proximity to Thrassus could result in memory loss and amnesia, as well as ratcheting up social tensions among the people around him. Possibly attracting outsiders (other Garou tribes, the Technocracy, Wyrm minions) to attack the sept overtly or clandestinely via some kind of dream summons or subtle influence. Such memory loss and amnesia can easily be associated with a Delerium effect which we already discussed in regards to Thrassus.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is actually very reasonable<br /> Oblivion (the thing the Wyrm often associated with) have a tendency to consume memories - and even memories are not safe from Entropy, the Wyrm main &quot;aspect&quot; so to speak<br /> Actually most of the time as times march on memory is the first to give in...<br /> As for the Delirium?<br /> The Tallons are ancient... but the Garou were already around to fight them so not<i> that </i>ancient<br /> Some Mokol\u00e9 have a Merit which causes them to inflict Delirium on Garou and other mammalian Shapeshifters as well, when in their Archid form... so having the/a Talons similar effect?<br /> Totally in the realms of possibilities and fits nicely in to the story<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-16T01:14:47-07:00","id":1524130,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524123#post1524123\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don\u2019t think any Talons are mentioned again. Bill Bridges steps down as developer of Werewolf, and Ethan Skemp takes over. It isn\u2019t until 1998 that the Book of the Weaver introduces Exhibit <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/node/338\" >#</a> 338 There we are told \u201ca few, pointing to same-time similarities between this item\u2019s discovery and the recent struggles at the Sept of Bygone Visions, whisper that it serves as prison to no less an entity than Thrassus the Thrice Damned, Smallest Talon of the Wyrm itself.\u201d</div> </div> </div> </div>It's unfortunate, though hardly surprising, that the whole Talons plot thread was effectively abandoned after its primary creative architect left the company. It happens a lot in corporate environments.<br /> <br /> That said, I can understand why White Wolf people would be reluctant to follow through on it after Bridges departed. If they weren't privy to whatever plans Bridges had, they'd only have what they already published as the basis for progressing the Talons plot. And there's not a great deal of meat on the bones, as written. At least, little that could be turned into actionable material for games. There's a bunch of imprisoned shards of the Wyrm's physical incarnation in the world? Sounds like the kind of thing you, as a writer, wouldn't want getting out, until you were ready to pull the trigger on the Apocalypse. And that doesn't help the scenario writers or Storytellers much.<br /> <br /> The idea the Talons might exert influence indirectly, despite being imprisoned, was evidently never considered by the folks at White Wolf. At least not after Bridges left.<br /> <br /> In the end, the best that was done was taking a stab at sowing seeds for more Talons plot, via Exhibit #338, a few years later. But nothing further was done. White Wolf didn't even bother tying those loose ends off during Revised, like officially killing off folks like the Tal'Mahe'Ra or the Children of Osiris (the vampire parts at least). The Talons just disappeared.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This implies that proximity to Thrassus could result in memory loss and amnesia, as well as ratcheting up social tensions among the people around him. Possibly attracting outsiders (other Garou tribes, the Technocracy, Wyrm minions) to attack the sept overtly or clandestinely via some kind of dream summons or subtle influence. Such memory loss and amnesia can easily be associated with a Delerium effect which we already discussed in regards to Thrassus. </div> </div> </div>It's nice to have another potential thread to follow with Thrassus's influence. That is, the loss of memory.<br /> <br /> Thrassus is a force of decay. This includes decay of recollection. The ties of cultural knowledge fray and disintegrate, until no one can remember why they stood vigil over a site. The souring of memory is especially bad for cultures who rely on rich oral traditions, like Garou.<br /> <br /> <br /> As an aside, I find the association of Thrassus with memory loss amusing, from a meta perspective. The Talon's capacity for inducing amnesia was so strong, the Talons of the Wyrm as a concept were memory-holed out of the minds of Werewolf in general. What's a more successful tool of the Wyrm than one everyone forgets until it's too late?<br /> <br /> (I've been doing a D&amp;D megadungeon creation challenge this year. I dedicated a chunk of it to a Mind Flayer lair. One aspect of my homebrew interpretation of Mind Flayers is they operate on <a href=\"https://tardis.fandom.com/wiki/The_Silence\" target=\"_blank\">The Silence</a> from Doctor Who rules. They can eat the thoughts, feelings, and memories right out of your head. So they can operate functionally invisibly, by passively eating peoples' perceptions of them as they form. It makes them way scarier, and justifies them being known by virtually no one in-universe.<br /> <br /> I imagine one could use that kind of idea for Thrassus. Some of its agents - Banes/Fomori - just walk around, unknown and unremarked upon, because they passively decay the memories of them in the minds of witnesses. Changeling 20th did a bit of this as well, with the Thallain &quot;Ghasts&quot;, whose victims don't recall the details of being attacked, and generally fly under the radar. Perhaps Thrassus has minions that fill a similar niche to those half-mortal minions of the Fomorians.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-16T12:23:40-07:00","id":1524145,"title":"","content":"I asked ExcelGarou to check if there were any more later references to the Talons in addition to the main sources we've been using for Book of the Weaver. While their master index isn't done yet, there are some extra mentions in Revised!<br /> <br /> <img width=\"378\" height=\"106\" data-align=\"none\" data-size=\"full\" loading=\"lazy\" border=\"0\" src=\"https://i.imgur.com/jx07KOi.png\" alt=\"\" data-fullsize-url=\"https://i.imgur.com/jx07KOi.png\" data-thumb-url=\"https://i.imgur.com/jx07KOi.png\" data-title=\"Click on the image to see the original version\" data-caption=\"\" class=\"bbcode-attachment thumbnail js-lightbox bbcode-attachment--lightbox\" itemprop=\"image\"/>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-16T13:19:57-07:00","id":1524150,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524130#post1524130\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That said, I can understand why White Wolf people would be reluctant to follow through on it after Bridges departed. If they weren't privy to whatever plans Bridges had, they'd only have what they already published as the basis for progressing the Talons plot. And there's not a great deal of meat on the bones, as written. At least, little that could be turned into actionable material for games. There's a bunch of imprisoned shards of the Wyrm's physical incarnation in the world? Sounds like the kind of thing you, as a writer, wouldn't want getting out, until you were ready to pull the trigger on the Apocalypse. And that doesn't help the scenario writers or Storytellers much.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep. Once a creative person leaves, then any unwritten ideas you have cannot be exploited. Only what was actually published. And if all you have is the idea is that this thing does nothing except pop up once the Apocalypse begins, it's an idea that is unhelpful to STs. The only thing you can do is have some bad guy attempt to find it (Azarea in Rite of Passage, Baba Yaga in Rage Across Russia). And that too is not very helpful. All scenarios should be designed with the idea of &quot;What happens if the PCs lose?&quot; because that is always an option. If the choice is only victory or the setting is destroyed and unrecognizably changed (because it is now the Apocalypse), it's bad because few STs want to completely alter their setting so radically. So it ends up being &quot;the PCs will win no matter what mistakes or failures they make&quot; which is just as bad. Players actually don't like that. They want their actions to matter, just that any mistakes can be recoverable in some way.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524130#post1524130\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The idea the Talons might exert influence indirectly, despite being imprisoned, was evidently never considered by the folks at White Wolf. At least not after Bridges left.</div> </div> </div> </div>That does puzzle me. It seems so obvious. The Call of Cthulhu RPG usually assumes the Great Old Ones are not active, but slumbering. But that doesn't stop various cults and creatures around them from doing things. I basically see the Talons of the Wyrm as a substitute for the Great Old Ones. That's why I try to build some kind of faction around these entities. That way they can be utilized during play without running a plot where either the PCs succeed or the world is destroyed. You need stakes that are less than that most of the time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524130#post1524130\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thrassus is a force of decay. This includes decay of recollection. The ties of cultural knowledge fray and disintegrate, until no one can remember why they stood vigil over a site. The souring of memory is especially bad for cultures who rely on rich oral traditions, like Garou.</div> </div> </div> </div>I had not considered the idea of memory damage really hurting the oral culture and traditions of the Garou. But it is an obvious angle to take. And it fits well with a lot of horror tropes. Each generation of Garou near a Thrassus site slowly having their memory degraded of what exactly is down there, or the rites needed to keep it imprisoned, or what to do in certain circumstances, can lead to a lot of interesting things. Nothing so much that the loss is noticeable or obvious, but it just happens so that after a few centuries what is known by the oral history is much degraded.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524130#post1524130\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Perhaps Thrassus has minions that fill a similar niche to those half-mortal minions of the Fomorians.</div> </div> </div> </div>You could create a brand new monster, fomori, or bane to do it; or simply make it something that all things belonging to Thrassus possesses. Simply make it a unique Power or Charm that requires an expenditure of Willpower or something else, and then consult the Delerium chart or make a Willpower roll to resist. It doesn't need to always happen. Just some of the time. It just takes some deft uses by the ST to create the proper atmosphere or obtain buy-in from the players.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-16T13:32:39-07:00","id":1524151,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524145#post1524145\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I asked ExcelGarou to check if there were any more later references to the Talons in addition to the main sources we've been using for Book of the Weaver. While their master index isn't done yet, there are some extra mentions in Revised!</div> </div> </div> </div>That's great to know. Thanks for sharing. I just looked up these references.<br /> <br /> Tribebook: Glasswalkers doesn't tell us anything about Talons. There's a search engine fetish on that page, and in its description it just mentions using it to find the location of a lost Talon of the Wyrm would be difficulty 10.<br /> <br /> The Players Guide to Garou just summarizes what is already known in four paragraphs on page 27. It mentions Narlthus and Koschei, and then says nothing is known about the other three Talons although at least one is possibly bound. That two Talons that are supposed to be guarded at the two Black Fury septs are just forgotten. Page 35 is just a speculation that the approach of Anthelios might be connected to the Talons.<br /> <br /> It's nice these Revised books had some references to the Talons as a concept, buy they don't tell us anything new or interesting. Still nice to know they are there just to make sure there isn't anything new.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-16T16:09:17-07:00","id":1524161,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524150#post1524150\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yep. Once a creative person leaves, then any unwritten ideas you have cannot be exploited. Only what was actually published. And if all you have is the idea is that this thing does nothing except pop up once the Apocalypse begins, it's an idea that is unhelpful to STs. The only thing you can do is have some bad guy attempt to find it (Azarea in Rite of Passage, Baba Yaga in Rage Across Russia). And that too is not very helpful. All scenarios should be designed with the idea of &quot;What happens if the PCs lose?&quot; because that is always an option. If the choice is only victory or the setting is destroyed and unrecognizably changed (because it is now the Apocalypse), it's bad because few STs want to completely alter their setting so radically. So it ends up being &quot;the PCs will win no matter what mistakes or failures they make&quot; which is just as bad. Players actually don't like that. They want their actions to matter, just that any mistakes can be recoverable in some way.</div> </div> </div> </div>It IS possible to involve a hunt for a Talon, if you assume the antagonist the PCs are racing against is barking up the wrong tree. That they just got their intel wrong, and the Talon isn't where they thought it was. (Or what's there is an unrelated thing).<br /> <br /> Using this kind of &quot;wild goose chase&quot; plot is of limited utility for Storytellers. You don't want players thinking they wasted their time too often. An occasional romp of little consequence isn't too bad. But you can't rely on that, lest the players stop caring. More often than not, the threat needs to be real. Which brings us around to the Talons, themselves, being a threat that's TOO real.<br /> <br /> I like the concept of the container of Thrassus leaking. That way, there's single drops of concentrated evil that can be separated from the whole, to be sought and fought over.<br /> <br /> One might have dripped on the ground and now is buried deep in the soil of a location. Poisoning the local groundwater and possibly gestating some subterranean horror. It's in the land, it's in the water. It's in the people who drink the water and consume the fruits of the land. It's below, and all around you. It eats at foundations of buildings, until they collapse into stinking sinkholes. It eats at the flesh, the mind, the memory. Causing cancers and blood poisoning and amnesia and domestic violence.<br /> <br /> One drop may have been consumed or injected into a person decades ago. In a plot to slowly incubate a spiritual evil within their bloodline. Really play into the Gothic Horror element, where some long-buried sin of the family causes physical, moral, spiritual, and mental decay over generations. If Thrassus, as a stand-in for the Prince of Darkness, is a kind of Anti-God, are its cultists attempting to create an Anti-Christ? (Or the Wyrmish equivalent thereof). Or did the drop of the Talon's essence grant power to the bloodline's founder, but this power comes at a terrible cost? Slowly corrupting, rotting the family from the inside out. Would eradicating this bloodline even be helpful, if so much of their blood literally spilt would simply release the corruption into the environment? Perhaps that's the long-term plan.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I had not considered the idea of memory damage really hurting the oral culture and traditions of the Garou. But it is an obvious angle to take. And it fits well with a lot of horror tropes. Each generation of Garou near a Thrassus site slowly having their memory degraded of what exactly is down there, or the rites needed to keep it imprisoned, or what to do in certain circumstances, can lead to a lot of interesting things. Nothing so much that the loss is noticeable or obvious, but it just happens so that after a few centuries what is known by the oral history is much degraded. </div> </div> </div>Importantly, decaying oral histories would cause more problems than simply hiding sites of the Talon's power. It also degrades bonds of friendship between packs, between Cairns and Septs, between whole Tribes. Connections of loyalty, friendship, mutual defense, and honor. All severed or become frayed. The Sept cannot be counted on to come to the aid of their allies, despite promises made and pacts forged in ages past to do so.<br /> <br /> This eroding of memory could even be selective. Groups of Garou forget the bonds of friendship, but remember grudges. Every positive interaction between groups or even individuals forgotten, while every slight, error, or insult endures. Love withers, while hate remains strong.<br /> <br /> It's an insidious weapon against the Wyrm's mightiest foes. The Garou need no help forgetting their duty and clashing with one another. But it's help Thrassus is more than happy to supply.<br /> <br /> <br /> (I'm reminded of a defunct webcomic called <a href=\"https://broodhollow.krisstraub.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Broodhollow</a>. About a small town playing host to some deep-seated, Lovecraftian force. One of the more insidious aspects of this force is how it modifies the memory of its inhabitants. People who were recent immigrants to the town are soon integrated so thoroughly, no one remembers they weren't <i>always</i> there. Complex systems of social rules and holidays are constructed, to maintain memory of past events (though the true significance thereof is often lost). And characters may investigate strange aspects of the town, only to stumble onto notes <i>written by themselves</i> showing the same discoveries, indicating they investigated it all before and simply forgot. It's really a shame the comic hasn't been updated in almost a decade.)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":6156,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","user":"Bluecho","date":". 10-16-2023, 10:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-17T12:05:01-07:00","id":1524202,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524161#post1524161\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This eroding of memory could even be selective. Groups of Garou forget the bonds of friendship, but remember grudges. Every positive interaction between groups or even individuals forgotten, while every slight, error, or insult endures. Love withers, while hate remains strong.<br /> It's an insidious weapon against the Wyrm's mightiest foes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ironically, Time and Entropy is the Wyrm's greatest weapons<br /> And the Tallons actually could be way older than anyone remembers - sure the Garou claim the Wyrm manifested a gigantic hand that corrupted everything it touched, until brave Garou warriors managed to rip the hand into shreds but his fingers had clawed themselves deep into Gaia - allegedly creating the great lakes in Canada - and managed to flee the Garou by changing their form<br /> This actually fits in nicelly with the scientific evidences - The Great Lakes began to form at the end of the Last Glacial Period around 14,000 years ago<br /> And Ways of the Wolf told us, the Garou were created before the Ice Age (and their life spans were closer to wolf years, but over time, they begin to have more human life spans)<br /> Shattered deams refined this a bit further, claiming 70, 000 years ago the supervolcano Mount Toba erupted in modern Indonesia. The eruption unleashed the Banestorm, permanently altering the Umbra and allowing the Garou to gain prominence among the Fera<br /> Funny to mention, the Toba Erruption almost made Humanity extinct - only a small group (about a thousand to ten thousand breeding pairs), with limited genetic variability, survived by chance in Africa<br /> Now here comes a little play ith numbers, just for fun!<br /> In the distant future, all evidence of the Human Race will be completely gone<br /> If a cataclysm wiped out the Human Race tomorrow, it would take just a few years for nature to reclaim urban areas<br /> In a few thousand years all cities are burried<br /> In 100.000 years all Human remains are fossilized<br /> In a million yeas all evidence of the Human Race will have mineralized; even styrofoam and plastic turns to stone and crumbles<br /> In 10 million years all that stone, the fossils and every bit of evidence of the entire Human Race is recycled back into the Earth's mantle<br /> We know this will happen becasue the oldest surface land ever discovered is the Negev Desert in Israel. It is about 1.8 million years old<br /> Thats it<br /> Every other piece of exposed land we've ever found is newer than that.<br /> Because of the Earth's plate tectonics today's mountans are yesterday's ocean floor. New land is formed every day as old lands are eroded in to dust.<br /> Thats why discovering fossils are a lot more difficult than people think!<br /> Very specific conditions need to be present for fossiliization.The organism needs hard body parts, like bones, teeth and shells. The remains need to be quickly covered and protected from scavenging and erosion. You need high pressure for mineralization and low oxygen to prevent decomposition.<br /> This almost never happens<br /> Dinosaurs roamed the Earth for about 180 million years; trillions upon trillions individual animals lived and died yet we only have a few thousand near complete fossils.<br /> Just for to help to graps the numbers, it is estimated that over two and hlf billion Tyrannosaurus Rexes loved and died on the Earth but fewer than 100 fossils have ever been found and<br /> only one of them is more or less complete!<br /> That means we've only discovered 0.0000004% of the dinosaur species<br /> Now a species as short-lived as Homo Sapiens might not be represented in the existing fossil record at all!<br /> The current area of urbanization is less than 1% of the Earth's surface. So Human artifacts like roads, cities, machines even megastructures would last only a few thousand years and are unlikely to ever be found<br /> Scientist conluded, direct evidences can only go back about 4 million years, even a nuclear war's evidence would dissappear eventually<br /> An archeologist far in the future would never know we even existed<br /> ...unless he finds and tests uranium (naturally uranium contains a very consistent and specific amount of isotope Uranium-235 and when U-235 is split, for a brief moment it absorbs a new neutron and is turned into U-236 then U-236 is split in to other elements like barim, krypton or strontium. Since all natural uranium decays at the same rate any uranium pulled out of the should have the same exact amount of U-235. The only way - literally the only way for uranium ore to have a lower percentage of U-235 is through nuclear fission)<br /> Evidence of our nuclear technology would still be detectable in uranium<br /> That future archeologist will be confused because even though there is no evidence of civilization or technology the uranium he finds will show evidence of fission<br /> Other scientists will come up with theoires about nuclear reactors that spontaneously appear in nature under perefct conditions<br /> But the archeologist feels there is more to the story<br /> He has a nagging sense that millions of years ago someone was here<br /> And who's to say that this hasn't already happened and that future archeologist<br /> ..is us?<br /> Just for fun, there is one such anomaly - the uranium in question came from a mine called Oklo. It's located in the former French colony of Gabon in Western Africa. It is at the moment, Earth's Two-billion-year-old only known natural nuclear reactor<br /> Either the impossible naturally happened... or some one two billion years ago toyed with nucler fission...<br /> Sorry to digress\u200b<br /> \u200bBack on topic!<br /> <br /> WoD is full of mysteries, like the Vhujunka or the Chulorviah and literally anything could be linked to the Tallons of the Wyrm<br /> Especially if we open the possibility of decaying memories and forgotten past<br /> Considering the Defiler Kings - a small group of survivors of the Lizard Kings, once powerful Wonder-Workers in their own right - turned to the Wyrm and became the first Mnetics? And the Innocents, the Wraith-like spirits of Mokol\u00e9 Metis always had the disturbing abilities: Steal Mnesis (One success: a memory within Mnesis becomes confused. Three successes: A minor memory is gone. Five successes: An ordinary memory is gone, or a great memory is blurred. Seven+ successes: \u00c0 great memory is destroyed.) and Corrupt Mnesis (The Innocent can change Mnesis...)?<br /> <br /> This box of Pandora, the awesome idea with Thrassus displaying similar abilites could be literally devastating to the Garou (and anyone else) - especially if the Weaver's servants (or anyone, really) tries to harness Thrassus/Exhibit # 338 &quot;power&quot; or refine it...<br /> It is a bit shame really the Game never explored this possibility in depths<br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","user":"Shadeprowler","date":". 10-17-2023, 03:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-18T22:47:07-07:00","id":1524257,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524161#post1524161\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like the concept of the container of Thrassus leaking. That way, there's single drops of concentrated evil that can be separated from the whole, to be sought and fought over.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was thinking about how frequent Thrassus might &quot;leak&quot; or manifest or otherwise affect an area with its entropy. I don't want it to be more prevalent than a trapped entity should be. But then I remembered I speculated about a less powerful Bane (than the Nexus Crawler) with the Warp Reality Charm. That Charm is perfect for justifying things like memory loss, time dilation, insect swarms, and other events associated with entropy and decomposition. So I would justify those things by having those minor Banes connected with Thrassus appearing.<br /> <br /> I just need to figure out a good name and image that is distinct from Nexus Crawlers and evocative with Thrassus. Any ideas would be appreciated.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524161#post1524161\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(I'm reminded of a defunct webcomic called <a href=\"https://broodhollow.krisstraub.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Broodhollow</a>. About a small town playing host to some deep-seated, Lovecraftian force.</div> </div> </div> </div>I remember reading that too. Great reference to Kris Straub. I only found out about that comic after I discovered the <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/@LOCAL58TV\" target=\"_blank\">Local 58</a> diegetic horror channel on YouTube and learned about Kris Straub's work for there. He does a lit of great stuff. Before I found Broodhollow, I found this YouTube video on his creepypasta fictional children's show <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VN0vSIBnUNo\" target=\"_blank\">Candle Cove</a>. This is someone's <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GWXW3jyjVAs\" target=\"_blank\">presentation of what that TV show</a> might be like. And this is <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bSaJEUB9oMw\" target=\"_blank\">an earlier versio</a>n. I think that could be very &quot;Thrassus&quot; as well.<br /> <br /> And the idea of some kind of madness inducing work of art or media, along with other themes of mental and spiritual breakdown, reminds of the King of Yellow and how plays, operas, music, poetry and other art works can create insanity and madness. So this may be another avenue of Thrassus that can be developed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 10-19-2023, 01:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-24T17:17:45-07:00","id":1524491,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524257#post1524257\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And the idea of some kind of madness inducing work of art or media, along with other themes of mental and spiritual breakdown, reminds of the King of Yellow and how plays, operas, music, poetry and other art works can create insanity and madness. So this may be another avenue of Thrassus that can be developed.</div> </div> </div> </div>In my last post I mentioned that some elements we\u2019ve created for Thrassus could be evocative of the Cthulhu Mythos entity Hastur and the King in Yellow. Common themes or degeneracy and decay leading to madness. So what kind of things might we take away from this? One common theme are artworks inspired by such mythos entities that cause madness in people. These are not occult tomes that must be studied by occultists. These are, on their surface, ordinary plays, music, paintings, etc. that reveal some truth of cosmic horror.<ul><li>The symbol of the Yellow Sign - anyone who possesses or sees \u2013 even by accident \u2013 a copy of the Sign is susceptible to some form of insidious mind control or possession</li> <li>The play King in Yellow - creates obsession and delusions</li> <li>The opera Massa Di Requiem Per Shuggay - provoked a riot</li> </ul>Over the centuries there have been works of art created by cultists or victims of Thrassus that have a strange and terrible effect on people. The physical works themselves are ordinary. Plenty of people have seen, read, or listened to them without incident. But something in their nature are portals to the essence of Thrassus. But since this is not universal, there are plenty of skeptics about such \u201cforbidden\u201d art works. We might take this further and say that when certain works are posted online that the digital versions somehow lack that portal. STs can always put that effect in, but I generally lean towards things that make it easier for the ST to explain why the Veil, Masquerade, etc. exists. <br /> <br /> The physical artifacts of such works are completely harmless. However, their creators somehow implanted the psychic residue of Thrassus in their works. How such a thing can be done is impossible for the rational human mind to understand. Given the right conditions when a manifestation of Thrassus intrudes upon reality, these works are triggered like time bombs when they come in contact with the human mind. Like hypnosis or a hallucination, they hijack the mind of some people who are spiritually senstive but lack sufficient psychic protection. The lack of protection could be because of low Willpower, a low level of the local Gauntlet, being Wyrm tainted, or have their natural defenses weakened by use of drugs or poison. Those who are considered to be Innocent in the World of Darkness are somehow immune to this, just as they are protected in other ways.<br /> <br /> But when triggered in certain minds, the person succumbs to madness.<br /> <br /> So now we only need to think of some iconic works that exist in the World of Darkness, or perhaps hijack some that may already exist in Mage or another sourcebook. Maybe there is a banned Qing Dynasty Chinese opera about the \u201clegendary\u201d Centipede King of Hang Chu (mentioned in Infernalism: the Path of Screams). Or there is a unique set of non-standard Tarot Cards printed in the 17<sup>th</sup> century (\u201cthe Florentine Tarot\u201d) that had its own strange card games and methods of divination. Arcana unknown in other forms of Tarot, all lavishly but obscenely illustrated. The original sets are all said to be destroyed, and it is now only known by a heavily expurgated version that is barely known. A Persian book of poetry of the 10th-11th centuries about Judgment Day, but so blasphemous that the ulema universally condemned and destroyed all copies (although lost versions always seem to be found). That kind of thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-27T23:38:05-07:00","id":1524620,"title":"","content":"With my association of Thrassus with certain elements of Hastur and the Yellow King, I am inspired by something in the Yellow Dawn RPG. For those who don\u2019t know it, it was an independent RPG setting in a post-apocalyptic world where the essence of Hastur has somehow embodied itself in Earth\u2019s reality. It\u2019s mainly a post-apocalypse survivor horror setting and the actual Hastur/Cthulhu Mythos elements are minimal.<br /> <br /> However, one aspect in are humans reduced to a pseudo-zombie status similar to what we see in the movie 28 Days Later with humans infected by the Rage virus. I think there are elements here that can be repurposed for us.<br /> <br /> The essence of Thrassus can infect some people who have a high spiritual sensitivty, low willpower, and are located in a place with a low Gauntlet. When this happens, the presence of Thrassus can overwhelm the mind and drive psychological changes in people. Their awareness expands as they perceive the overlapping layers of reality - the physical, the penumbral, the cthonic, the astral, and others - all at the same tme. Their experience becomes an utterly confusing, non-Euclidean, chaotic meta-reality. Victims become incoherent. It breaks down their sense of self, and they become in thrall to Thrassus.<br /> <br /> Besides expanding their consciousness, contact with Thrassus also gives them Rage. They become utterly psychotic as they enter the Thrall of the Wyrm. These people lose themselves in an existence of horror and rage. From the perspective of others, the people seem to be having schizophrenic hallucinations and become incredibly aggressive and strong, like users of drugs like PCP.<br /> <br /> They do not become fomori. Other than possessing one (or in rare cases more) point of Rage, they have no supernatural powers. They just become psychotic monsters. Their cognitive and personality centers of the brain shut done, and they become frenzied and aggressive. Per the Thrall of the Wyrm, they are driven to attack, kill and \u201cconsume\u201d their prey. This is not from hunger, mere compulsion. As a result, the vast majority of victims engage in biting or gnawing their victims, but do not actually cannibalize them.<br /> <br /> This is not a disease or spirit possession. Nor can victims pass their \u201cinfection\u201d to another. It is the human mind succumbing to the psychic presence of Thrassus. When some aspect of Thrassus manifests somewhere, it contaminates the place and the spirituallysensitive.<br /> <br /> These attacks are extremely rare, but have been recorded in every part of the world in every culture throughout history. Every culture interprets these attacks in ways that make sense to them, often involving supernatural causes, demonic possession, or witchcraft. Modern psychiatry refers to it as Spontaneous Induced Rage Personality Syndrome (SIRPS), and understand it by reference to various stress and personality factors creating schizophrenic hallucinations. SIRPS does not appear to be inheritable or the result of infection. It remains a medical mystery.<br /> <br /> Historically, most victims end up beging killed by unaffected humans. But as psychiatrists better understand the syndrome, they are developing ways to help their patients. Currently some patients diagnosed with SIRPS are being kept in induced comas and heavy depressants after being subdued. There has been some success in integrating restrained patients back to sanity. The biomedical company DNA is a pioneer of medical research in this area, but has only had limited success in curing victims.<br /> <br /> Garou recognize the victims, whom they refer to as the Rabid, as a sympton of the intrusion of Thrassus upon the world, and it usually provokes local septs to begin an investigation as to the location and type of Thrassus\u2019 manifestation and the reason for its arrival. Some Garou believe that during the Apocalypse when Thrassus finally escapes its fetish prison that large segments of the human population will succumb and could push civilization into collapse.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-27T23:53:59-07:00","id":1524621,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524620#post1524620\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With my association of Thrassus with certain elements of Hastur and the Yellow King, I am inspired by something in the Yellow Dawn RPG. For those who don\u2019t know it, it was an independent RPG setting in a post-apocalyptic world where the essence of Hastur has somehow embodied itself in Earth\u2019s reality. It\u2019s mainly a post-apocalypse survivor horror setting and the actual Hastur/Cthulhu Mythos elements are minimal.<br /> <br /> However, one aspect in are humans reduced to a pseudo-zombie status similar to what we see in the movie 28 Days Later with humans infected by the Rage virus. I think there are elements here that can be repurposed for us.<br /> <br /> The essence of Thrassus can infect some people who have a high spiritual sensitivty, low willpower, and are located in a place with a low Gauntlet. When this happens, the presence of Thrassus can overwhelm the mind and drive psychological changes in people. Their awareness expands as they perceive the overlapping layers of reality - the physical, the penumbral, the cthonic, the astral, and others - all at the same tme. Their experience becomes an utterly confusing, non-Euclidean, chaotic meta-reality. Victims become incoherent. It breaks down their sense of self, and they become in thrall to Thrassus.<br /> <br /> Besides expanding their consciousness, contact with Thrassus also gives them Rage. They become utterly psychotic as they enter the Thrall of the Wyrm. These people lose themselves in an existence of horror and rage. From the perspective of others, the people seem to be having schizophrenic hallucinations and become incredibly aggressive and strong, like users of drugs like PCP.<br /> <br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Between Delirium turned on Garou to make them forget bonds and duties, and Rage spliced on humans to reduce them to mindless berserkers, making a mockery out of the Garou seems an unintended theme for Thrassus. Nice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-28T15:24:16-07:00","id":1524663,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524621#post1524621\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Between Delirium turned on Garou to make them forget bonds and duties, and Rage spliced on humans to reduce them to mindless berserkers, making a mockery out of the Garou seems an unintended theme for Thrassus. Nice.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's probably because I like to use existing game mechanics that can be repurposed than invent new house rules of my own. It keeps things easier to remember and run during the game.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-29T17:24:44-07:00","id":1524719,"title":"","content":"Meant to talk about this a while back, but if we're discussing Hastur, I might recommend last year's indie horror darling <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Signalis\" target=\"_blank\">SIGNALIS</a>. After all, the player finds The King In Yellow within minutes of starting the game. So while the overt influence of Hastur isn't clear, there's very obviously an association there.<br /> <br /> I don't want to get too into spoilers, as Signalis is a pretty deep and interesting game, with lots of emotionally resonant moments. But elements present in the game include: people being liquified and coalescing deep underground as a cancerous mass of flesh; the protagonist losing big chunks of time (as if their memory or perception of reality is unreliable); people (robots this time) devolving into distorted, violent zombies; visions of other places and times; <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E.Y.E.:_Divine_Cybermancy\" target=\"_blank\">&quot;cycles of guilt&quot;</a>, etc. As befits a game taking heavy inspiration from series like Resident Evil and Silent Hill.<br /> <br /> If you want to play it yourself, it's obviously on <a href=\"https://store.steampowered.com/app/1262350/SIGNALIS/\" target=\"_blank\">Steam</a> and other outlets.<br /> <br /> If you don't want to play it yourself, but want to experience it and its plot points, I can recommend a <a href=\"https://youtu.be/7RuyKT7Q0OE?si=_gFlrmSVivLPW5Bc\" target=\"_blank\">Let's Play by Bricky</a>.<br /> <br /> If you don't have time to watch a whole Let's Play, <a href=\"https://youtu.be/86UvzTdnpvA?si=bpqlym4ut39aU4H-\" target=\"_blank\">Charlatan Wonder did a good (albeit spoiler-full) overview</a>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-29T19:46:14-07:00","id":1524730,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1524491#post1524491\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> In my last post I mentioned that some elements we\u2019ve created for Thrassus could be evocative of the Cthulhu Mythos entity Hastur and the King in Yellow. Common themes or degeneracy and decay leading to madness. So what kind of things might we take away from this? One common theme are artworks inspired by such mythos entities that cause madness in people. These are not occult tomes that must be studied by occultists. These are, on their surface, ordinary plays, music, paintings, etc. that reveal some truth of cosmic horror.<ul><li>The symbol of the Yellow Sign - anyone who possesses or sees \u2013 even by accident \u2013 a copy of the Sign is susceptible to some form of insidious mind control or possession</li> <li>The play King in Yellow - creates obsession and delusions</li> <li>The opera Massa Di Requiem Per Shuggay - provoked a riot</li> </ul>Over the centuries there have been works of art created by cultists or victims of Thrassus that have a strange and terrible effect on people. The physical works themselves are ordinary. Plenty of people have seen, read, or listened to them without incident. But something in their nature are portals to the essence of Thrassus. But since this is not universal, there are plenty of skeptics about such \u201cforbidden\u201d art works. We might take this further and say that when certain works are posted online that the digital versions somehow lack that portal. STs can always put that effect in, but I generally lean towards things that make it easier for the ST to explain why the Veil, Masquerade, etc. exists.<br /> <br /> The physical artifacts of such works are completely harmless. However, their creators somehow implanted the psychic residue of Thrassus in their works. How such a thing can be done is impossible for the rational human mind to understand. Given the right conditions when a manifestation of Thrassus intrudes upon reality, these works are triggered like time bombs when they come in contact with the human mind. Like hypnosis or a hallucination, they hijack the mind of some people who are spiritually senstive but lack sufficient psychic protection. The lack of protection could be because of low Willpower, a low level of the local Gauntlet, being Wyrm tainted, or have their natural defenses weakened by use of drugs or poison. Those who are considered to be Innocent in the World of Darkness are somehow immune to this, just as they are protected in other ways.<br /> <br /> But when triggered in certain minds, the person succumbs to madness.<br /> <br /> So now we only need to think of some iconic works that exist in the World of Darkness, or perhaps hijack some that may already exist in Mage or another sourcebook. Maybe there is a banned Qing Dynasty Chinese opera about the \u201clegendary\u201d Centipede King of Hang Chu (mentioned in Infernalism: the Path of Screams). Or there is a unique set of non-standard Tarot Cards printed in the 17<sup>th</sup> century (\u201cthe Florentine Tarot\u201d) that had its own strange card games and methods of divination. Arcana unknown in other forms of Tarot, all lavishly but obscenely illustrated. The original sets are all said to be destroyed, and it is now only known by a heavily expurgated version that is barely known. A Persian book of poetry of the 10th-11th centuries about Judgment Day, but so blasphemous that the ulema universally condemned and destroyed all copies (although lost versions always seem to be found). That kind of thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>On the subject of works of art that induce madness in their audience, we can take some inspiration from two works I can think of off the top of my head.<br /> <br /> First, we have <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In_the_Mouth_of_Madness\" target=\"_blank\">In the Mouth of Madness</a>, another of John Carpenter's &quot;Apocalypse Trilogy&quot; along with the previously discussed Prince of Darkness and The Thing. It's about a horror writer whose writings become a vessel for The Old Ones to return to Earth, by driving people mad, mutating them, and eroding the veil of reality. It's said near the start of the film that the effects are at least initially contained to &quot;less stable readers&quot; (though this obviously ceases to be contained later). Causing disorientation, memory loss(!), and paranoia (and, as the protagonist discovers, later ballooning into physical mutation, homicidal violence, and/or suic*de).<br /> <br /> Parallels to HP Lovecraft's body of work is all but explicit, and very much intended by the director. Lining up its imagery and plot to Thrassus is a no-brainer, and it's appropriate given the other work in this &quot;Trilogy&quot; that directly inspired Thrassus to begin with.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Second, there's <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Videodrome\" target=\"_blank\">Videodrome</a>, an 80s Canadian horror film about pirated videos of apparent (and, as it turns out, real) snuff films unfolds into a mind-conspiracy of &quot;socio-political warfare&quot; in North America. Where works of psycho-sexual violence beget more violence in their viewers, and which also has a distinctly body-horror element. Whether these are true mutations (or warps in reality), or simply hallucination. Videodrome is also where we get the line &quot;Long Live the New Flesh&quot;, which is something to chew on.<br /> <br /> There's a bit of an element of this &quot;technology&quot;, the titular Videodrome, being used for different purposes depending on who used it. (It's creator was killed after he discovered his partners were using it for malevolent purposes). So if we crib ideas from it, we could examine how a similar tool might be employed by Thrassus cultists, rather than being a force of independent, self-perpetuating malignancy (although I doubt any form of it could ever be benign).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Speaking of self-perpetuating malignancy, though, we might as well bring up the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Ring_(franchise)\" target=\"_blank\">Ring</a> franchise. A story about a cursed videotape that kills its viewers in 7 days (except when the timeframe is different) unless they copy the tape and have it viewed by someone else. In the series, the tapes were created by a psychic girl named Sadako (with a different name in other languages), who was murdered and now uses the tapes to spread knowledge of her demise. (Later novels attempted to establish a more Sci Fi explanation, involving a virus, but I find this less satisfying than it just being a curse).<br /> <br /> This concept can easily be ported over to Thrassus. A cursed work that not only kills or drives its audience mad, but must be spread to others to avoid the worst fate. Turning the work from not merely a spiritial landmine, but an active artistic contagion. Each person having to spread it to the next to escape death. Possibly leaving them &quot;merely&quot; insane for their trouble. Slowly deepening the spiritual corruption in the world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-29T21:53:39-07:00","id":1524734,"title":"","content":"I've been trying to figure out a way to include elements from Videodrome in WtA for a while now, but have never been satisfied with my musings. I think it's more of a PENTEX style thing than cosmic horror associated with Thrassus, but I understand wanting to include it from a media perspective. Likewise, I associate the Ring more with Wraith and spectres than WtA, but the elements of weird media inspired horror fits. (Plus I don't think being a Wraith thing means it is inappropriate for WtA. Of all the game lines, I think it (along with Mage) is the most crossover friendly.)<br /> <br /> Like with many things, it all depends on how you implement the inspiration. Certainly with how Thrassus is being developed on this thread, we can find appropriate elements.<br /> <br /> I think the Mouth of Madness allusions are more appropriate. Although anything like the events of the movie would best be used in an actual &quot;the Apocalypse has begun&quot; chronicle than as an ordinary element of a chronicle that has the Apocalypse happening sometime in the future. Someone like Sutter Cane having access to the Periphery, but under the subtle influence of Thrassus in his works would work as a chronicle element.<br /> <br /> In other news, I have decided on a name for the Banes associated with Thrassus that have the Warp Reality charm, but are less powerful than Nexus Crawlers. I will be calling them Degensites. A portmanteau of degenerate and genesis. A breaking down of the creation of the world. I'll probably give them stats like Rage 4, Gnosis 6, Willpower 4. Just enough to justify weird events, but not enough to make them unbeatable for low ranked Garou PCs.<br /> <br /> Like with all my points, I encourage people to do their own thing. While I am developing Thrassus and its minions to work in my own chronicles, steal whatever you like to work within your own ideas for your own chronicles. Simply because I don't like something or am more attracted to a certain direction doesn't mean you shouldn't take what you like and do something different. These threads are meant to be a toolkit for you to use.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-06T22:29:53-08:00","id":1525155,"title":"","content":"I now wonder if there should be any connection between Thrassus and the NPC of Hao Ker (the Centipede King) mentioned in 1998's Infernalism: the Path of Screams. It's a Mage: the Sorcerer's Crusade sourcebook that I utilize a lot. Centipedes match the vermin concept we've talked a lot about in this thread, and I believe other sourcebooks match the Wyrm to the centipede. So I think it may fit. The Centipede King ruled the fictional Hang Chu province in China around the year 1410.<br /> <br /> When talking about Thrassus as an ongoing and persistent threat to Gaia and the Garou, I think it is important to identify specific events in history that the Garou battled, defeated, or identified the threat of Thrassus. That provides an actual history that can make the threat more real. What other events can be assigned to a specific time and place to make this more real? When were the pig-men first discovered? Were the Get of Fenris who first arrived in Greenland wiped out by &quot;Skraelings&quot; who were in fact Thrassus fomori? How did the threat manifest in the Western Hemisphere among the Pure Ones, or around Tibet for the Stargazers?<br /> <br /> I hope some people can provide ideas of what they think would be interesting in game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-08T02:08:10-08:00","id":1525197,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1525155#post1525155\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When were the pig-men first discovered?</div> </div> </div> </div>I couldn't really say when the first pig-men were created, but I imagine you could do some neat things with <a href=\"https://youtu.be/6WkjHyKHyX4?si=Gpq35--NsAN_oIYT\" target=\"_blank\">the introduction of the Domesticated Pig to the Americas by the Spanish</a>.<br /> <br /> A species first brought as a food source and tool of colonialism, pigs were dispersed in many Caribbean islands, and later the mainland. Often small numbers were left behind on islands by the Spanish, knowing by the time they came back later the population would have exploded all on its own. For use as ready-made food for colonists or marooned sailors. Even natives at the time would begin raising them.<br /> <br /> That is, when pigs weren't escaping captivity and breeding in the wild. Some Spanish explorers reported finding wild hogs that had reached places before the explorers themselves had.<br /> <br /> Simply the presence of mundane pigs was an ecological disaster for the &quot;New World&quot;. Islands stripped bare of plants and fruit, that had no natural defenses against the pig. Moreover, European swine brought all manner of diseases and parasites with them, causing mass death (when they weren't killing natives through starvation from ecological collapse). Pigs are STILL a troubling invasive species in the Americas. In the US, it's often pig hunting season year round, just to thin their numbers.<br /> <br /> Combine all this with the fact pigs can survive on anything, including human feces, and we've got plenty of data points to start concocting some pig-men action. Hell, the introduction of pigs to the Americas might, in and of itself, be part of the Wyrm's plan to cause mass death. Especially in regions that Garou have presence and/or Kinfolk. Humans didn't need encouragement to bring their bacon with them, but it certainly furthered the Wyrm's interests.<br /> <br /> Imagine an island suffering ecological collapse. The people - whether native or colonists - become susceptible to the influence of Thrassus due to the stress of their situation. One or more start feeding the dead (or the dying) to their pigs, or leaving them staked at the edge of the jungle as offerings to wild hogs (or to Bane spirits that dwell among them). The transgression converts swine into swine-men. Still largely contained by the sea, but able to do lots of damage nonetheless.<br /> <br /> Perhaps some swine-men shamen - Wyrm-priests and Thrassus-speakers - take up where their makers left off. Using foul rites to whip up storms, poison the ocean, draw humans to them via corrupt dream magic (creating totems that act as signal boosters for Thrassus in the material world), or even shroud their island in a mental fog that causes it to escape mapping or trade routes. All while lesser pig-men maybe hop into rafts or canoes, to make swift night-time raids on surrounding islands. Claiming supplies and prisoners, for use as food, sacrifice, or &quot;breeding stock&quot; (though this last you might be inclined to de-emphasize for the sake of good taste).<br /> <br /> Through the forgetting-magic, the island might escape notice for centuries. (Who knows? Maybe this has something to do with the &quot;Bermuda Triangle&quot;.). The island becomes visited by pirates (vintage or modern) who stumble upon it by accident while looking for hideouts from the law. Or by settlers, castaways, or tourists looking to party. (Imagine a &quot;Fyre Fest&quot; style event, whose organizers bring their guests en masse to an island on no map by mistake. With no infrastructure in place for rescue to even find the island, let alone effect aid, the people are picked off one by one by the pig-men.)<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Were the Get of Fenris who first arrived in Greenland wiped out by &quot;Skraelings&quot; who were in fact Thrassus fomori? </div> </div> </div>Given that Greenland is mostly a frozen wasteland, you could easily take more than a little inspiration from John Carpenter's The Thing. (Which, funnily enough, is the THIRD part of Carpenter's &quot;Apocalypse Trilogy&quot;).<br /> <br /> The Get of Fenris might investigate, or even become involved in, an outpost where a particular kind of Thrassus Bane is infecting people and turning them into a shape-shifting Fomori under the Bane's control. The Get need to eradicate them, before the infection can spread to other settlements or back to mainland Europe/America.<br /> <br /> This might be a &quot;period adventure&quot;, occurring centuries past. Or alternatively, the Get of Fenris might have contained the threat during their original arrival long ago, at the cost of their lives. And now their successors in the Tribe discover the Bane and its &quot;Thing-Fomori&quot; manifesting at an isolated research base that undercovered what the previous Get had buried.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-08T10:46:37-08:00","id":1525211,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1525197#post1525197\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Combine all this with the fact pigs can survive on anything, including human feces, and we've got plenty of data points to start concocting some pig-men action. Hell, the introduction of pigs to the Americas might, in and of itself, be part of the Wyrm's plan to cause mass death. Especially in regions that Garou have presence and/or Kinfolk. Humans didn't need encouragement to bring their bacon with them, but it certainly furthered the Wyrm's interests.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like the idea that the arrival of feral swine in the Americas could have been the trigger for the creation of the swine things, and I have a great idea of who was behind it. The Voodoo Pigs. Originally the Skull Pigs were some Ice Age species of North American tapirs and peccaries that were corrupted into monsters before the Pure Ones arrived. These monsters were thus indigenous to North America. Later the writers forgot this part and simply decided to make them corrupted descendants of the Grondr - wrong species, wrong continent, wrong timeline. So if you go with that retcon in your chronicles, this may not work for you. But with the original lore you have these intelligent, magic using Voodoo Pigs who now have access to some new, unknown species creating ecological upheaval. They could have created the first pig men in secret completely isolated from any human contact, and then recruited the first human cultists from any scavenging human survivors of a people wiped out by disease, by criminal exiles, or other social outcasts on the fringes of human society.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1525197#post1525197\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Through the forgetting-magic, the island might escape notice for centuries. (Who knows? Maybe this has something to do with the &quot;Bermuda Triangle&quot;.). The island becomes visited by pirates (vintage or modern) who stumble upon it by accident while looking for hideouts from the law.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like the idea that there may have been Thrassus cultists among the Caribbean pirates. Pirates were made up of all sorts of outcasts which is where the cult is strongest. You could invent some sort of pirate lore concerning a particularly awful pirate who performed horrible deeds (actually one of the worst fomori or Ferectoi of the cult). Steal what you can from actual pirate history, supernatural stuff from Disney's Pirates of the Caribbean, and even a legend concerning some &quot;buried treasure&quot; that in fact should never be found. Just need to come up with a great fictional pirate name and spin a whole legend around him. There's a lot to work with. Maybe the Brujah pirate Smiling Jack once knew him or served on his ship?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1525197#post1525197\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Given that Greenland is mostly a frozen wasteland, you could easily take more than a little inspiration from John Carpenter's The Thing. (Which, funnily enough, is the THIRD part of Carpenter's &quot;Apocalypse Trilogy&quot;).</div> </div> </div> </div>I selected Greenland because 1) Lovecraft mentioned Greenland as one of those fringe places with a known Cthulhu Cult, 2) the term skraeling has as one of its possible meanings &quot;wretched one&quot; which sounds a lot like fomori, and 3) the disappearance of the Norse settlement of Greenland is one of those things that can easily lend itself to supernatural explanations. So a Thrassus cult that formed among exiles/criminals/outcasts on Greenland wiping out a small and isolated band of Garou and kinfolk fits those ideas.<br /> <br /> Whether the Get managed to defeat the threat at the cost of their own lines and send word to their brethren back east (perhaps a kinfolk fleeing east or the last Get survivor leaving by the last use of a moonbridge from a minor caern now being abandoned), or just have late arriving Wendigo end the threat (or perhaps some other group like Dreamspeakers) is all up to whatever the ST prefers. In any case, I think a doomed sept of Get of Fenris - no matter how small - helps create the idea that Thrassus is an actual threat that can defeat even great warriors of the Garou.<br /> <br /> I do like your idea there is some buried threat under the Greenland ice. That provides nice story seeds that can be used in any number of ways.<br /> <br /> I remembered some Get and Wendigo ancestor spirits mentioned in Axis Mundi that had some early contact while these Garou were alive, and just checked to see if they could somehow be included as part of this. But it turns out that they met in Vinland, not Greenland, and their meeting would have been in the 11th century while the Norse settlements in Greenland would not disappear until sometime in the early 15th century. But an ST could use the family line of Ragnar Red Axe as later involved in this encounter to provide more details on what happened and build a small little history of a minor and forgotten Get of Fenris sept on the shore of Greenland.<br /> <br /> In any case, I hope others provide some more ideas of encounters. The more specific details, the more it makes the menace seem real.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 11-08-2023, 01:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-01T15:54:17-08:00","id":1526213,"title":"","content":"Been away from the forums for a while, but my curiosity was piqued when I saw the thread title referring to Thrassus Thrice-Damned. I always found the entry in the Book of the Weaver on Exhibit 338 to be fascinating; just its very nature as a transparent container made of &quot;not-glass&quot; holding an unknown viscous substance was something I found very compelling for some reason. Anyway, on a lark some years ago, I tried to do a short write-up on the Talons of the Wyrm and put it in my GoogleDocs in the hopes that someone might find it useful for inspiration. Keep in mind, I tried to find all mentions of the Talons of the Wyrm in W:tF books, but I only found references in <i>Rite of Passage</i>, <i>Rage Across Russia, Caerns: Places of Power </i>and <i>Book of the Weaver</i>. My research was cursory and might be incomplete. Since my write-up is not that long, I'll copy and paste it below. For the record, none of this is canon; it's all just spitballing on my part. I also included a couple of paragraphs on the nature of the remaining two Talons that were never detailed by White Wolf. I just made up the names Vothnaak and Galchus and tried to imagine what sort of artifacts would contain the last two Talons of the Wyrm. In particular the information on the Tain being one of the artifacts stored at the Sept of Bygone Visions is stuff I totally made up. Anyway, to wit:<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><u><span style=\"font-family:Balthazar\">The Five Talons of the Wyrm</span></u></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><u>Narlthus, Thumb (or First Talon) of the Wyrm</u>: referenced in <i>Rite of Passage</i>. Currently held by a sept of Black Spiral Dancers, led by Azaera. It has not yet been released from its egg-shaped meteorite prison. Reputed to be the Talon of Beast-of-War.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><u>Koschei the Deathless, the First Finger (or Second Talon) of the Wyrm, &quot;He-Who-Points&quot;</u>: referenced in <i>Rage Across Russia</i>. Currently bound in the Soul Egg, a gemstone held by a sept of Uktena Banetenders in Russia. Reputed to be the Talon of Eater-of-Souls.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><u>Thrassus Thrice-Damned, Smallest- (or Fifth) Talon of the Wyrm</u>: referenced in <i>Book of the Weaver</i>. Currently imprisoned in the Anti-Christ Serum, labeled in a classified storage facility as \u201cExhibit 338,\u201d location unknown (but maybe in Roswell, NM in Area 51?). The object imprisoning Thrassus Thrice-Damned appears to be a three-foot long, seamless glass capsule (but if the transparent material is indeed glass, it is of a type of glass that cannot be scratched or marred by any man-made instrument) containing a thick blue-green fluid of unknown substance. As the Smallest Talon, Thrassus Thrice-Damned may be the Talon of the Defiler Wyrm, but this is only non-canon speculation.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">The Sept of the Bygone Visions entry in <i>Caerns: Places of Power</i> had no references to an imprisoned Talon of the Wyrm. The only other reference to the Sept of the Bygone Visions mentioning the Talons of the Wyrm was in <i>Book of the Weaver</i> suggesting that since the discovery of the Anti-Christ Serum happened at the same time as &quot;recent struggles at the Sept of Bygone Visions&quot; the two incidents were somehow related which in turn suggested that the Anti-Christ Serum was Thrassus Thrice-Damned. I found another reference to the unnamed Talon held in the Sept of Bygone Visions on page 97 of <i>Rage Across Russia</i> in the same section describing Koschei.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><i>World of Darkness: Blood-Dimmed Tides</i> makes reference on page 16 to an unnamed Talon of the Wyrm lying buried somewhere in the Arctic Circle. The Garou of the Wendigo tribe believe that this Talon of the Wyrm lies hidden underneath the half-frozen silt, guarded by terrible aquatic monsters and Banes.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">Assuming there are five Talons of the Wyrm, that accounts for all of them. Either the Third or Fourth Talon could be contained in the vault of the Sept of Bygone Visions.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">It seems more than likely that all of the Talons have been imprisoned as their existence would have come to light long ago if any one of them were still free....</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><u>Unofficial write-ups for the Third and Fourth Talons (feel free to use or modify this information for inspiration)</u>:</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">\u2022 Galchus, also known as \u201cHe-Who-Scorns\u201d and the Third/Middle Talon of the Wyrm, is buried in the frozen silt of the Arctic Ocean. Long ago, when Galchus was still free and fleeing from the Garou, he was pursued by a pack of Wendigo warriors and Uktena Banetenders. The Uktena had brought from the south with them two artifacts that were to be used to imprison Galchus: a stone sarcophagus of a unique and unknown design (any human archaeologist who tried to identify the origins of the sarcophagus would be utterly baffled) and a simple sewing needle made from whale bone. Once the pack had defeated Galchus, the surviving Garou performed a Rite to bind Galchus within the needle, then sealed the needle inside the sarcophagus and sunk the sarcophagus beneath the frigid waves of the Arctic Ocean. Over time, the sarcophagus sank into the silt, where it has remained ever since. Now, thousands of years later, a number of aquatic monsters and Banes surround the area, as if vigilantly trying to protect the Talon. The Wendigo still tell half-remembered tales of a Talon of the Wyrm sunk beneath the waves in the far north, but Galchus\u2019 name, his location and even the manner of his binding have been lost to history.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">\u2022 Vothnaak, Fourth Talon of the Wyrm, is imprisoned inside an artifact called the Tain, which appears to be a very old and strange-looking jewel box. The Tain is one of the many artifacts contained in the Womb, the hidden vault of artifacts located at the Black Fury Sept of Bygone Visions on the island of Miria (known to the modern world as Ecube). The Sept of Bygone Visions do not know that a Talon of the Wyrm is imprisoned inside one of their stored artifacts. Vothnaak\u2019s name, the tale of how he came to be imprisoned within the Tain and even the tale of how the Tain came to be stored inside the Womb have all been lost to time. It is up to the Storyteller to decide if Iona Kinslayer\u2019s vision of a shadow spreading across the Glen of Miria has anything to do with Vothnaak.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">Assumed Directions that the Talons of the Wyrm fled (assuming they \u201clanded\u201d in Europe):</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">Koschei ran to the East(-Northeast) to Russia.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">Narlthus ran to the North(west) to Canada.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">Thrassus Thrice-Damned ran in an unknown direction. Assuming that Thrassus Thrice-Damned is located at Area 51 (and not any other undisclosed location), then it may have run towards the (American) Southwest (assuming it was bound near that area and not later transported to Area 51).</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">Galchus ran to the North to the Arctic Circle.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">Vothnaak ran to the Southeast to Greece (assuming it got captured and bound near Greece).</span><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> \u200bI hope someone finds this useful.<br /> \u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/835/7xu8.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/546/wpur.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/folderview?id=0By9UuUWWH6CiMVlhWHMwNWcyaG8&amp;usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Complete Garou Gifts List, Fera Gifts List, Garou Totems List and other assorted OPP/White Wolf goodies</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2710-su-tehp","name":"Su-tehp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2710_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"532"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}